<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jing Gu
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>School of Mathematics, University of Minnesota</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Morteza Mardani
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>NVIDIA</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Wonjun Lee
</span><span id="id3" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Department of Mathematics, The Ohio State University</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Dongmian Zou
</span><span id="id4" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Zu Chongzhi Center and DIRC, Duke Kunshan University</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Gilad Lerman<span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotemark"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotemark: </span>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
          
          
          
          
        </span></span></span>
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models often degrade when trained in latent spaces (e.g., VAEs), yet the formal causes remain poorly understood. We quantify latent-space diffusability through the rate of change of the Minimum Mean Squared Error (MMSE) along the diffusion trajectory. Our framework decomposes this MMSE rate into contributions from Fisher Information (FI) and Fisher Information Rate (FIR). We demonstrate that while global isometry ensures FI alignment, FIR is governed by the encoder’s local geometric properties. Our analysis explicitly decouples latent geometric distortion into three measurable penalties: dimensional compression, tangential distortion, and curvature injection. We derive theoretical conditions for FIR preservation across spaces, ensuring maintained diffusability. Experiments across diverse autoencoding architectures validate our framework and establish these efficient FI and FIR metrics as a robust diagnostic suite for identifying and mitigating latent diffusion failure.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id5" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models have revolutionized generative modeling by transforming noise into structured data through a learned reverse process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. While these models excel in high-dimensional pixel spaces, computational constraints often necessitate their application within the compressed latent spaces of pre-trained autoencoders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. However, a persistent and poorly understood phenomenon remains: diffusion models often degrade or fail entirely when applied directly to the latent spaces of standard autoencoders, including Variational Autoencoders (VAEs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. Recent literature refers to this mismatch between diffusion dynamics in pixel space and latent space as the <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">diffusability</em> problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Current explanations for this failure typically point to the “geometric distortion” introduced by standard encoders, which warp the data manifold and create regions that diverge from the Gaussian prior assumed by the diffusion process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. In response, various empirical methods have emerged to mitigate this issue, such as geometry-preserving adaptations (e.g., Geometry Preserving Encoder/Decoder (GPE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>), explicit target latent geometries <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, and modified training objectives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. (We provide a comprehensive discussion of these related works in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Related Works ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite these empirical advances, the underlying causes of latent diffusion failure remain theoretically obscured. Crucially, the literature lacks a formal, quantitative framework for measuring how “diffusible” a latent space is <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">before</em> committing to the computationally expensive training of a full diffusion model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we demystify latent-space diffusability through the rigorous lens of Fisher geometry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, complementing recent information-geometric perspectives on diffusion trajectories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. We analyze diffusability via <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">denoising complexity</em>, quantified by the rate of change of the Minimum Mean Squared Error (MMSE) along the continuous diffusion trajectory. We show that this denoising complexity fundamentally decomposes into two terms: the Fisher Information (FI) and its rate of dissipation, which we define as the Fisher Information Rate (FIR).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To match pixel-space diffusability, encoders must preserve both FI and FIR. Stabilizing FI requires global near-isometry (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), while stabilizing FIR imposes stricter constraints: the encoder must be locally near-isometric and approximately flat (Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Fisher Information Rate and Second-Order Distortion ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>). Critically, our analysis explicitly decouples the penalties of intrinsic geometric distortion (local curvature and nonlinearity) from the baseline effects of dimensional compression.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our main contributions are as follows:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theoretical Framework:</span> We formalize the relationship between latent diffusability, denoising complexity (MMSE), and Fisher Information Geometry.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Geometric Bounds:</span> We derive the explicit local curvature and isometry conditions necessary for FIR stability, mathematically separating geometric distortion from dimensional compression.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical Diagnostic Suite:</span> Extensive experiments on toy and FFHQ datasets
validate our efficient FI and FIR metrics as robust predictors of latent diffusion performance. We show that unlike GPE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, standard VAEs exhibit severe FIR deviations that correlate with generation failure.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Score-based Diffusion Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Continuous-time score-based models transform a data distribution into a noise prior. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> be a Borel probability measure on <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> concentrated on a low-dimensional manifold <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}\subset\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\subset\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider a diffusion process parameterized by the noise variance <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau:=\sigma^{2}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau:=\sigma^{2}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, where a clean sample <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}\sim\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}\sim\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is corrupted by standard Gaussian noise <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{n}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{n}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{D})</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding the noisy observation <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{x}_{0}+\sqrt{\tau}\mathbf{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>τ</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐧</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{x}_{0}+\sqrt{\tau}\mathbf{n}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The marginal density of these noisy observations, known as the <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">heat-smoothed</em> density <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the corresponding <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">probability flow ODE</em> evolve as:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial p_{\tau}}{\partial\tau}=\frac{1}{2}\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\quad\text{and}\quad d\mathbf{x}=-\frac{1}{2}\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})d\tau." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial p_{\tau}}{\partial\tau}=\frac{1}{2}\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\quad\text{and}\quad d\mathbf{x}=-\frac{1}{2}\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})d\tau.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Generating samples requires a neural approximation of the <em id="S2.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">score function</em></p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Learning <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is challenging because the intrinsic geometry of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> induces high-frequency variations in directions normal to the manifold.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Latent Diffusion and the Geometry Gap</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To reduce computational costs, diffusion is often performed in the compressed latent space <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> of a pre-trained autoencoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. However, standard VAEs regularize <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> toward a Gaussian prior using Kullback-Leibler divergence, which frequently causes “manifold crinkling” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. This phenomenon occurs when distant points on the data manifold <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> are mapped to adjacent latent coordinates to satisfy the prior, ensuring that Euclidean distances in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> fail to preserve the intrinsic data geometry. This creates a fundamental “geometry gap” between the spaces. Recent architectures address these geometric distortions by explicitly regularizing latent representations. For example, Geometry-Preserving Encoders (GPEs) align latent Euclidean geometry with data-space Euclidean geometry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, while spherical and manifold autoencoders utilize non-Euclidean latent spaces to better capture complex data topologies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>The Diffusability Issue</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A distribution’s <em id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">diffusability</em>—its ease of modeling via diffusion—depends on the score function’s smoothness and learnability. Structural irregularities or low-density regions cause poorly calibrated gradients, degrading sample fidelity.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Spectral Artifacts in Latent Spaces.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compressed latent spaces often exacerbate these irregularities. Spectral analyses show autoencoders introduce high-frequency components or singularities, impeding coarse-to-fine synthesis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. Even with smoother encodings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, diffusion remains unstable if the encoded manifold in <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> lacks structural regularity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations of Metric Regularization.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent techniques constrain the encoder Jacobian to enforce local isometry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. While empirically addressing the geometry gap, they lack a formal information-geometric analysis of diffusability. To address this gap, we leverage Fisher geometry to establish a rigorous link between encoder geometry and denoising complexity, explaining why certain latent spaces remain difficult to diffuse despite geometric-preservation properties.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Information-Geometric Formalism</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We review two Fisher-information metrics. We address two different scenarios of a Borel probability measure <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>: 1) The setting of Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Score-based Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, where <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=D</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is concentrated on a manifold <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}\subset\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\subset\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>; 2) The setting inspired by Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Latent Diffusion and the Geometry Gap ‣ 2 Background ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, with a latent space of dimension <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&lt;D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&lt;D</annotation></semantics></math>, an encoder <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E:\mathbb{R}^{D}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E:\mathbb{R}^{D}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> the pushforward measure of the former <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> according to <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. In both settings, for a noise variance <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the <em id="S2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">heat-smoothed</em> measure <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\tau}:=\mu*\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\tau\mathbf{I}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\tau}:=\mu*\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\tau\mathbf{I}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> admits a strictly positive, smooth probability density <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>. Its score <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2.1 Score-based Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Whenever <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> are clear from context, we may denote our metrics without the dimension superscript, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the input measure, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S2.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Fisher Information</em> (FI) of <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is its square-averaged score and the <em id="S2.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Fisher Information Rate</em> (FIR) is the dissipation rate of FI. That is,</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}\equiv\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau}):=\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}}\left[\|\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\|^{2}\right]\quad\text{and}\quad\mathcal{R}\equiv\mathcal{R}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau}):=-\frac{d}{d\tau}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}\equiv\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau}):=\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}}\left[\|\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\|^{2}\right]\quad\text{and}\quad\mathcal{R}\equiv\mathcal{R}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau}):=-\frac{d}{d\tau}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The next proposition establishes an exact representation of FIR, which emerges across information theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> calculus for diffusion semigroups <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, and optimal transport <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. We provide a proof in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Proof of Proposition ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Derivations and Proofs ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>. Let <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\tau}=\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\tau}=\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Hessian representation of FIR)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})=\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}}\left[\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\|_{F}^{2}\right]=\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}}\left[\mathrm{Tr}(\mathbf{H}_{\tau}^{2})\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})=\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}}\left[\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\|_{F}^{2}\right]=\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}}\left[\mathrm{Tr}(\mathbf{H}_{\tau}^{2})\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Computational Efficiency.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the FIR involves the Frobenius norm of the score Jacobian, it remains computationally tractable for high-dimensional neural networks. Using the Hutchinson trace estimator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, we approximate the norm as <math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(\tau)=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x},\mathbf{v}}[\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{v}\|^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(\tau)=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x},\mathbf{v}}[\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{v}\|^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is a random noise vector. The term <math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is efficiently evaluated as a Jacobian-vector product (JVP) in a single forward-mode pass, bypassing the need to explicitly compute or store the full Jacobian matrix. See Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Estimation of FI and FIR ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> for more details.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To formalize “diffusability,” we analyze the sensitivity of the global Minimum Mean Squared Error (MMSE) to <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. Following the setting of Section <a href="#S2.SS4" title="2.4 Information-Geometric Formalism ‣ 2 Background ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, where either <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=D</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=d</annotation></semantics></math>, the error in estimating the clean signal <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from its noisy observation <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> at variance <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{MMSE}(\tau)=\mathbb{E}[\|\mathbf{x}_{0}-\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{x}_{0}|\mathbf{x}]\|^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>MMSE</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{MMSE}(\tau)=\mathbb{E}[\|\mathbf{x}_{0}-\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{x}_{0}|\mathbf{x}]\|^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The I-MMSE identity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, proved in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Derivation of () and () ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Derivations and Proofs ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>, establishes that the optimal denoising performance is fundamentally constrained by this information content:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{MMSE}(\tau)=\tau k-\tau^{2}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>MMSE</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{MMSE}(\tau)=\tau k-\tau^{2}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Differentiating (<a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) and applying the definition of FIR yields the decomposition:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underbrace{\frac{d}{d\tau}\text{MMSE}(\tau)}_{\text{Denoising Resistance}}=\underbrace{k-2\tau\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})}_{\text{Intrinsic Noise Gain}}+\underbrace{\tau^{2}\mathcal{R}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau}).}_{\text{Geometric Complexity Penalty}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>MMSE</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Denoising Resistance</mtext></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Intrinsic Noise Gain</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Geometric Complexity Penalty</mtext></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underbrace{\frac{d}{d\tau}\text{MMSE}(\tau)}_{\text{Denoising Resistance}}=\underbrace{k-2\tau\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})}_{\text{Intrinsic Noise Gain}}+\underbrace{\tau^{2}\mathcal{R}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau}).}_{\text{Geometric Complexity Penalty}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This decomposition reveals that denoising difficulty is governed by two distinct factors: the <em id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Intrinsic Noise Gain</em>, which drives a baseline linear degradation, and the <em id="S3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Geometric Complexity Penalty</em>, which causes errors to compound quadratically at higher noise scales, as noted by the term <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. To ensure a latent space <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is as “diffusable” as the original data space <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, an encoder must preserve both terms of this decomposition. In Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Global Isometry and Fisher Preservation ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, we establish the first-order conditions required to preserve the Intrinsic Noise Gain. In Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Fisher Information Rate and Second-Order Distortion ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we establish the second-order conditions required to bound the Geometric Complexity Penalty.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Global Isometry and Fisher Preservation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure the latent space <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> inherits the denoising characteristics of the data space <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, the encoder <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> must tightly bound the metric distortion of the Fisher Information, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math>. This ensures the <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Intrinsic Noise Gain</em> in (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) reflects genuine data complexity rather than artifacts of latent stretching or compression.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Fisher Information Bounds for Bi-Lipschitz Encoders)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}\subset\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\subset\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional smooth manifold and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E:\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E:\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth map. Assume the Jacobian of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly bounded in directions tangent to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, there exist constants <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c,C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c,C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\,\|\mathbf{v}\|\leq\|J_{E}(\mathbf{x})\,\mathbf{v}\|\leq C\,\|\mathbf{v}\|,\quad\forall\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{M}\text{ and tangent vectors }\mathbf{v}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and tangent vectors </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\,\|\mathbf{v}\|\leq\|J_{E}(\mathbf{x})\,\mathbf{v}\|\leq C\,\|\mathbf{v}\|,\quad\forall\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{M}\text{ and tangent vectors }\mathbf{v},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq d\leq D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq d\leq D</annotation></semantics></math>. If the intrinsic manifold gradient of the log-volume element vanishes at <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathcal{M}}\log\sqrt{\det(J_{E}(\mathbf{x})^{\top}J_{E}(\mathbf{x}))}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathcal{M}}\log\sqrt{\det(J_{E}(\mathbf{x})^{\top}J_{E}(\mathbf{x}))}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then the manifold FI on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{(D)}=\mathbb{E}[\|\nabla_{\mathcal{M}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\|^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{(D)}=\mathbb{E}[\|\nabla_{\mathcal{M}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\|^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>, is comparable with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{C^{2}}\mathcal{I}^{(D)}\leq\mathcal{I}^{(d)}\leq\frac{1}{c^{2}}\mathcal{I}^{(D)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{C^{2}}\mathcal{I}^{(D)}\leq\mathcal{I}^{(d)}\leq\frac{1}{c^{2}}\mathcal{I}^{(D)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Equivalence (<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{(d)}=\mathcal{I}^{(D)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{(d)}=\mathcal{I}^{(D)}</annotation></semantics></math>) occurs when <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is an isometric embedding (<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=C=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=C=1</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 3.1 (Fisher Information Bounds for Bi-Lipschitz Encoders). ‣ 3.1 Global Isometry and Fisher Preservation ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> shows that a near-isometric mapping (<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\approx C\approx 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>≈</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\approx C\approx 1</annotation></semantics></math>) neutralizes first-order geometric distortion. Thus, the shift in baseline denoising error between <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> reduces purely to the dimensionality gap, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D-d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D-d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Fisher Information Rate and Second-Order Distortion</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The dominant factor in diffusion difficulty at higher noise levels is the <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Geometric Complexity Penalty</em> governed by the FIR, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math>. High-frequency manifold crinkling or structural irregularities inflate the score Jacobian <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\mathbf{s}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\mathbf{s}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, causing denoising errors to compound quadratically as indicated by the factor <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). Therefore, effective diffusion requires keeping this penalty low.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Crucially, the FIR is mathematically driven by the magnitude of the score Jacobian (the Hessian of the log-density). To ensure this penalty is not artificially inflated by the encoder, we analyze how the encoder alters these second-order derivatives. Applying the chain rule to the score transformation <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x})=J_{E}(\mathbf{x})^{\top}\mathbf{s}_{\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x})=J_{E}(\mathbf{x})^{\top}\mathbf{s}_{\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{z})</annotation></semantics></math>, the data-space Hessian <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> relates to the latent-space Hessian <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> via:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{x}}=\underbrace{J_{E}(\mathbf{x})^{\top}\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}J_{E}(\mathbf{x})}_{\text{Inherited Curvature}}+\underbrace{\sum_{i=1}^{d}[\mathbf{s}_{\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{z})]_{i}\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{x}}(E_{i})}_{\text{Curvature Injection}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Inherited Curvature</mtext></munder><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Curvature Injection</mtext></munder></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{x}}=\underbrace{J_{E}(\mathbf{x})^{\top}\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}J_{E}(\mathbf{x})}_{\text{Inherited Curvature}}+\underbrace{\sum_{i=1}^{d}[\mathbf{s}_{\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{z})]_{i}\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{x}}(E_{i})}_{\text{Curvature Injection}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This decomposition reveals that even if the latent distribution is perfectly flat (<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}=\mathbf{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}=\mathbf{0}</annotation></semantics></math>), a non-linear encoder injects artificial curvature via its own second derivatives, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{x}}(E_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{x}}(E_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, while a ReLU activation is 1-Lipschitz (stable at the first order), its second derivative contains a Dirac delta; this non-smooth “kink” introduces a score singularity that drives the FIR toward infinity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Because this injected curvature directly inflates the latent FIR, we need a metric to quantify how much the encoder distorts the natural geometric complexity of the data. To formalize this, we define the relative deviation between the FIR of the data space and the FIR of the latent space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(FIR Deviation)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=D</annotation></semantics></math>, the intrinsic dimension of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{Z}:=E_{\#}\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{Z}:=E_{\#}\mu</annotation></semantics></math> be its pushforward under <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu_{Z})_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu_{Z})_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> denote these distributions diffused at noise variance <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">FIR deviation</em> <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> and its <em id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dimension-normalized</em> counterpart <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>sc</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> are:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}(\tau):=\left|1-\frac{\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})}{\mathcal{R}^{(d)}((\mu_{Z})_{\tau})}\right|,\qquad\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}(\tau):=\left|1-\left(\frac{d-m}{D-m}\right)\frac{\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})}{\mathcal{R}^{(d)}((\mu_{Z})_{\tau})}\right|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>sc</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}(\tau):=\left|1-\frac{\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})}{\mathcal{R}^{(d)}((\mu_{Z})_{\tau})}\right|,\qquad\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}(\tau):=\left|1-\left(\frac{d-m}{D-m}\right)\frac{\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})}{\mathcal{R}^{(d)}((\mu_{Z})_{\tau})}\right|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Stability Theorems on Flat Support</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To bound <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>sc</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>, we decouple the encoder’s artificial curvature from the data’s intrinsic curvature by assuming the data lies on a flat, low-dimensional subspace. This isolates the encoder’s geometric impact in two stages: first, quantifying baseline metric distortion with a linear encoder, and second, bounding the exact curvature penalty of nonlinear encoders.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Flat Support and Linear Mapping</span>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\subset\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\subset\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>–dimensional linear subspace with an isometry <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}:\mathbb{R}^{m}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}:\mathbb{R}^{m}\to M</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu:=(\mathbf{U}_{\#}\rho)\otimes\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu:=(\mathbf{U}_{\#}\rho)\otimes\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be a probability measure on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq d\leq D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq d\leq D</annotation></semantics></math>, and fix an isometry <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}:\mathbb{R}^{m}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}:\mathbb{R}^{m}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume the encoder <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E:\mathbb{R}^{D}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E:\mathbb{R}^{D}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is linear on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\mathbf{U}\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{V}(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐔𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐀𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\mathbf{U}\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{V}(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> for an invertible <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Linear Stability)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Assumption 3.3 (Flat Support and Linear Mapping). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{Z}:=E_{\#}\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{Z}:=E_{\#}\mu</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the near-isometry condition for some <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{A}^{\top}\mathbf{A}-\mathbf{I}_{m}\|_{2}\leq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐀</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{A}^{\top}\mathbf{A}-\mathbf{I}_{m}\|_{2}\leq\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\equiv C(m)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\equiv C(m)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=d=D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=d=D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}(\tau)\leq C\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}(\tau)\leq C\delta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&lt;d\leq D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&lt;d\leq D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}(\tau)\leq C\left(\frac{D-d}{D-m}+(d-m)\delta\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>sc</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}(\tau)\leq C\left(\frac{D-d}{D-m}+(d-m)\delta\right)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 (Linear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> isolates the first-order distortion (<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>), practical encoders are nonlinear. By imposing a global Taylor remainder bound, we constrain the Frobenius norm of the curvature injection term in expectation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Nonlinear Stability)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let the subspace <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and probability measure <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> be defined as in Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Assumption 3.3 (Flat Support and Linear Mapping). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, but let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E:\mathbb{R}^{D}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E:\mathbb{R}^{D}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be a nonlinear map. Assume <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and satisfies the Taylor residual bound:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\mathbf{x}_{0}\in M}\int_{M}\|E(\mathbf{x})-(E(\mathbf{x}_{0})+\nabla E(\mathbf{x}_{0})(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_{0}))\|^{2}\,d\mu(\mathbf{x})\leq\varepsilon^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mo>∫</mo><mi>M</mi><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em">⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\mathbf{x}_{0}\in M}\int_{M}\|E(\mathbf{x})-(E(\mathbf{x}_{0})+\nabla E(\mathbf{x}_{0})(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_{0}))\|^{2}\,d\mu(\mathbf{x})\leq\varepsilon^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If each local Jacobian <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}=\nabla E(\mathbf{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}=\nabla E(\mathbf{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{A}_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}^{\top}\mathbf{A}_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}-\mathbf{I}_{m}\|_{2}\leq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{A}_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}^{\top}\mathbf{A}_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}-\mathbf{I}_{m}\|_{2}\leq\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, then for some <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(m)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(m)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=d=D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=d=D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}(\tau)\leq C(\delta+\varepsilon/\sqrt{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>τ</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}(\tau)\leq C(\delta+\varepsilon/\sqrt{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&lt;d\leq D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&lt;d\leq D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}(\tau)\leq C\left(\frac{D-d}{D-m}+(d-m)\delta+\frac{\varepsilon}{\sqrt{\tau}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>sc</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>ε</mi><msqrt><mi>τ</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}(\tau)\leq C\left(\frac{D-d}{D-m}+(d-m)\delta+\frac{\varepsilon}{\sqrt{\tau}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Geometric Interpretation of Encoding Distortion.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Nonlinear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> reveals that FIR deviation splits into three distinct penalties: a <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">dimension mismatch</span> (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D-d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D-d</annotation></semantics></math>), a <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">tangential distortion</span> (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>), and a <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">nonlinearity penalty</span> (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon/\sqrt{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>τ</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon/\sqrt{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These distortions are visualized in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Geometric Interpretation of Encoding Distortion. ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. To maintain data-level diffusability, an encoder must act as a local isometry (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\approx 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\approx 0</annotation></semantics></math>) and exhibit low curvature (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\approx 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\approx 0</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure">
