_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F1.pic1.8" class="ltx_text">linear approx. region</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -116.2 -227.87)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.05em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="457.28"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F1.pic1.9" class="ltx_text">(c) Curvature Injection: high-frequency nonlinearity with large <math id="S3.F1.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and small <math id="S3.F1.pic1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Geometric interpretation of encoder assumptions.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The High-Frequency Penalty (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon/\sqrt{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>τ</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon/\sqrt{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span> Heat diffusion at variance <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> smooths the distribution over a spatial scale of order <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>τ</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>. At small <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> (low noise), the spatial smoothing scale is tiny. Consequently, high-frequency geometric distortions—such as the tight curvature shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Geometric Interpretation of Encoding Distortion. ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(c)—heavily disrupt the local geometry, driving up the FIR deviation via the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon/\sqrt{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>τ</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon/\sqrt{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> term.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the diffusion process naturally blurs out these tight geometric kinks, mitigating the penalty. The ratio <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon/\sqrt{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>τ</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon/\sqrt{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> effectively measures whether the encoder’s artificial curvature survives the “diffusion lens.” The FIR framework thus provides a rigorous, task-independent metric to evaluate how well an encoder preserves intrinsic geometric dynamics across all noise scales.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To apply the nonlinear stability guarantees of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Nonlinear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>, we must verify that a learned embedding can actually control both the local Jacobian distortion <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and the global nonlinear remainder <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. The following proposition demonstrates that encoders trained via the GPE loss inherently satisfy these structural conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\subset\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\subset\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact subset of an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional linear subspace with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in M</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{M}=\sup_{\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}\in M}\|\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{y}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{M}=\sup_{\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}\in M}\|\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{y}\|</annotation></semantics></math> denote its diameter. Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\in C^{2}(\mathbb{R}^{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\in C^{2}(\mathbb{R}^{D})</annotation></semantics></math> be an encoder achieving a GPE loss <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(E)&lt;\epsilon_{\mathrm{GPE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>GPE</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(E)&lt;\epsilon_{\mathrm{GPE}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{\mathbf{x}\in M}\|\nabla^{2}E(\mathbf{x})\|_{2}\leq L_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>sup</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{\mathbf{x}\in M}\|\nabla^{2}E(\mathbf{x})\|_{2}\leq L_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the conditions of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Nonlinear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> with local distortion and nonlinear remainder bounds:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=C_{1}\epsilon_{\mathrm{GPE}}^{\frac{1}{4(m+5)}},\quad\varepsilon=\left(\frac{L_{E}^{2}D_{M}^{4}}{4}\epsilon_{\mathrm{GPE}}^{1/4}+C_{2}\,\min\left\{L_{E}^{2},1+\epsilon_{\mathrm{GPE}}^{\frac{1}{4(m+5)}}\right\}\right)^{1/2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>GPE</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>M</mi><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>GPE</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>GPE</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=C_{1}\epsilon_{\mathrm{GPE}}^{\frac{1}{4(m+5)}},\quad\varepsilon=\left(\frac{L_{E}^{2}D_{M}^{4}}{4}\epsilon_{\mathrm{GPE}}^{1/4}+C_{2}\,\min\left\{L_{E}^{2},1+\epsilon_{\mathrm{GPE}}^{\frac{1}{4(m+5)}}\right\}\right)^{1/2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span>(On the Magnitude of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As the empirical loss <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\mathrm{GPE}}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>GPE</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\mathrm{GPE}}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the local Jacobian distortion <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> strictly vanishes, whereas the nonlinear remainder bound converges to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C_{2}\min\{L_{E}^{2},1\})^{1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C_{2}\min\{L_{E}^{2},1\})^{1/2}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-type distance losses (including the GPE encoder loss) do not inherently suppress high-frequency spatial oscillations. Thus, the uniform Hessian bound <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is not analytically forced to zero by the loss functional alone. To handle arbitrary variational pathologies where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{E}\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{E}\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the global geometric term <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> acts as an absolute upper ceiling, ensuring the remainder never exceeds the intrinsic spatial variance of the data manifold. However, because the encoder <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is parameterized by a neural network, the mapping is heavily regularized by the implicit spectral bias of gradient-based optimization, which strongly penalizes high-frequency modes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. In practice, the learned embedding possesses high global regularity, rendering the Hessian spectral norm <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> strictly small. We emphasize that while this spectral regularization is widely observed empirically, this argument currently serves as a heuristic. A rigorous justification for bounding <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> through the optimization dynamics of the network architecture remains an open question for future work.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, we compare FI, FIR, and FIR deviations computed from diffusion models trained in pixel space and in latent spaces produced by various encoders. We begin with Gaussian toy experiments and simple encoders to verify the theoretical results in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. We then move to real-world image data and more complex autoencoders, including GPE, VAE, and NVAE. For all experiments, implementation details, including model architectures, training configurations, and sampling parameters, are provided in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>FI and FIR Comparison in the Gaussian Toy Setting</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the Gaussian toy setting, we study how FI and FIR vary for tiny diffusion models trained on the data and on different encodings of the data.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a two-dimensional Gaussian dataset and its pointwise nonlinear encodings. Specifically, we generate training data <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and construct encoded samples <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, where the encoder <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is applied pointwise to each coordinate of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then train the tiny diffusion model<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              We use the open-source implementation available at <a href="https://github.com/tanelp/tiny-diffusion" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/tanelp/tiny-diffusion</a>.</span></span></span> with identical architecture and training settings separately on the original data <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and the encoded data <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We evaluate the FI and FIR using the trained diffusion models.
Details of the numerical estimation procedures are provided in
Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Estimation of FI and FIR ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/gaussian_FI.png" id="S4.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/272;" width="476" height="272" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/gaussian_FIR.png" id="S4.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/gaussian_relu_FI.png" id="S4.F2.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/272;" width="476" height="272" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/gaussian_relu_FIR.png" id="S4.F2.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/272;" width="476" height="272" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(d) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>
Values of <math id="S4.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> (left) and <math id="S4.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> (right) plotted versus the noise variance <math id="S4.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, computed from tiny diffusion models trained on different data representations. <em id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Pixel</em> curves correspond to models trained on <math id="S4.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. <em id="S4.F2.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Latent</em> curves correspond to models trained on encoded data <math id="S4.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, where the pointwise activation <math id="S4.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is indicated in the legend; for Leaky ReLU, <math id="S4.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the negative slope.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2 </span>Results</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4.1 FI and FIR Comparison in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> compares FI (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>) and FIR (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>) computed using diffusion models trained on original data <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> (denoted <em id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pixel</em> in the legend) versus encoded data <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> (denoted <em id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">latent</em>). We consider encoders <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> given by common activation functions: ReLU, Leaky ReLU (with negative slope <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>), GELU, sigmoid, and tanh. Derivations for the theoretical properties of encoders, using Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 3.1 (Fisher Information Bounds for Bi-Lipschitz Encoders). ‣ 3.1 Global Isometry and Fisher Preservation ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and discussion in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Fisher Information Rate and Second-Order Distortion ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, are provided in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Gaussian Toy Experiment: Derivations and Additional Results ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Comparison of FI across different encoders relative to the pixel baseline is shown in Figures <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4.1 FI and FIR Comparison in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(a) and (c). ReLU exhibits the largest deviation, consistent with Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 3.1 (Fisher Information Bounds for Bi-Lipschitz Encoders). ‣ 3.1 Global Isometry and Fisher Preservation ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, for which the derived FI upper bound diverges in the ReLU case (see Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Gaussian Toy Experiment: Derivations and Additional Results ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>). In contrast, smooth activations (GELU, sigmoid, tanh) are locally bi-Lipschitz on the Gaussian data support, and their FI curves remain closer to the pixel baseline. For Leaky ReLU, as <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> approaches <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, the mapping becomes closer to an isometry, and the FI curve correspondingly moves toward the pixel baseline.
We note that for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=2</annotation></semantics></math>, the FI curves are not strictly below the pixel baseline as suggested by Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 3.1 (Fisher Information Bounds for Bi-Lipschitz Encoders). ‣ 3.1 Global Isometry and Fisher Preservation ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, which may be due to numerical estimation error since FI is computed empirically from finite samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Comparison for FIR is shown in Figures <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4.1 FI and FIR Comparison in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(b) and (d).
ReLU exhibits the largest deviation from the pixel baseline, consistent with the discussion in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Fisher Information Rate and Second-Order Distortion ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, since it does not satisfy second-order regularity.
Among the smooth activations (GELU, tanh, sigmoid), deviations are smaller, with activations of lower curvature tending to produce smaller deviations.
Although Leaky ReLU does not satisfy the second-order regularity assumption, the observed deviation is not excessively large. We believe this is because <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is estimated from finite data, and the Leaky ReLU encoder can be effectively approximated by a smoother mapping on the data support. As <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> approaches <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, the smooth approximation has lower curvature, and the observed deviation decreases accordingly.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>FIR Deviation in the Gaussian Toy Setting</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study the behavior of FIR deviation in the Gaussian toy setting, and examine how FIR deviation is affected by perturbations of the data representation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider an intrinsic manifold of dimension <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=2</annotation></semantics></math> with data <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. We embed this into a higher-dimensional ambient space as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, and construct latent representations <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D,d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D,d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the same tiny diffusion model, training protocol, and FIR estimation procedure described in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 FI and FIR Comparison in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we compute <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(d)}((\mu_{Z})_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(d)}((\mu_{Z})_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math> using diffusion models trained on <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, and use these values to compute the FIR deviations <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>sc</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/gaussian_FIR_deviation_delta2.png" id="S4.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/289;" width="476" height="289" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/gaussian_FIR_deviation_d2.png" id="S4.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/292;" width="476" height="292" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F3.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/gaussian_FIR_deviation_epsilon2.png" id="S4.F3.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/292;" width="476" height="292" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F3.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/GPE_FIR_deviation.png" id="S4.F3.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/290;" width="476" height="290" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(d) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>
FIR deviation <math id="S4.F3.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. (a) <math id="S4.F3.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, (b) <math id="S4.F3.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, and (c) <math id="S4.F3.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in toy settings.
Data <math id="S4.F3.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> are embedded as <math id="S4.F3.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and encoded to <math id="S4.F3.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We compute <math id="S4.F3.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> from
<math id="S4.F3.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F3.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(d)}((\mu_{Z})_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(d)}((\mu_{Z})_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math>
using diffusion models trained on <math id="S4.F3.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F3.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Curves denote fixed noise variance <math id="S4.F3.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. Solid lines <math id="S4.F3.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1.25\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1.25</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1.25\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (a) and <math id="S4.F3.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=\frac{D-d}{D-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=\frac{D-d}{D-2}</annotation></semantics></math> (b) serve as linear references.
Encoder <math id="S4.F3.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> setups:
(a) <math id="S4.F3.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=d=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=d=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F3.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐀𝐱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.F3.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}=\mathrm{diag}(\sqrt{1+\delta_{0}},\sqrt{1-\delta_{0}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}=\mathrm{diag}(\sqrt{1+\delta_{0}},\sqrt{1-\delta_{0}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(b) <math id="S4.F3.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=512</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.F3.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
(c) <math id="S4.F3.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=d=3</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.F3.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E((x_{1},x_{2},0),\varepsilon_{0})=\bigl(\sin(\varepsilon_{0}x_{1})/\varepsilon_{0},\;x_{2},\;(1-\cos(\varepsilon_{0}x_{1}))/\varepsilon_{0}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E((x_{1},x_{2},0),\varepsilon_{0})=\bigl(\sin(\varepsilon_{0}x_{1})/\varepsilon_{0},\;x_{2},\;(1-\cos(\varepsilon_{0}x_{1}))/\varepsilon_{0}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>. 
<br class="ltx_break">(d) Real data (Sec. <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>): <math id="S4.F3.m53" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>sc</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S4.F3.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.F3.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=64\times 64\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=64\times 64\times 3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F3.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=256</annotation></semantics></math>). FFHQ images <math id="S4.F3.m57" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> are encoded to latents <math id="S4.F3.m58" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> via GPE or VAE (see legend).
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.2 </span>Results</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all experiments in this section, detailed derivations of the theoretical parameters using Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 (Linear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Nonlinear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>, and additional plots of the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> versus noise variance <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> are provided in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Gaussian Toy Experiment: Derivations and Additional Results ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>. We begin with the case <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=d=m=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=d=m=2</annotation></semantics></math>. The encoded data are obtained via a linear transformation <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐀𝐱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}=\mathrm{diag}(\sqrt{1+\delta_{0}},\sqrt{1-\delta_{0}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}=\mathrm{diag}(\sqrt{1+\delta_{0}},\sqrt{1-\delta_{0}})</annotation></semantics></math>. In this setting, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{A}^{\top}\mathbf{A}-\mathbf{I}\|_{2}=\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐀</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{A}^{\top}\mathbf{A}-\mathbf{I}\|_{2}=\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the near-isometry condition in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 (Linear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> is satisfied with deviation parameter <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4.2 FIR Deviation in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(a) shows <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for fixed noise variances <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each fixed <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> increases approximately linearly with respect to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, consistent with Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 (Linear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, which predicts <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}(\tau)\leq C\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}(\tau)\leq C\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that we report curves at relatively small noise variances <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, as for large <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> the FIR values become very small due to heavy smoothing, making the deviation numerically unstable and less informative.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we consider <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=512</annotation></semantics></math> and varying latent dimension <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. We take the latent representation <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, the encoder acts as the identity on the intrinsic subspace, from which we derive that the linear deviation parameter in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 (Linear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> satisfies <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=0</annotation></semantics></math>. The theorem then yields the bound for scaled FIR deviation <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}\leq C\frac{D-d}{D-m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>sc</mi></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}\leq C\frac{D-d}{D-m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4.2 FIR Deviation in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(b) reports <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>sc</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of latent dimension <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Consistent with our predicted bound, the curves exhibit an almost linear downward trend.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we consider <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=d=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x},\varepsilon_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x},\varepsilon_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\mathbf{x},\varepsilon_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\mathbf{x},\varepsilon_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is a nonlinear embedding that wraps the 2D data manifold into a 3D cylinder of radius <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\varepsilon_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\varepsilon_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E((x_{1},x_{2},0),\varepsilon_{0})=\bigl(\sin(\varepsilon_{0}x_{1})/\varepsilon_{0},\;x_{2},\;(1-\cos(\varepsilon_{0}x_{1}))/\varepsilon_{0}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E((x_{1},x_{2},0),\varepsilon_{0})=\bigl(\sin(\varepsilon_{0}x_{1})/\varepsilon_{0},\;x_{2},\;(1-\cos(\varepsilon_{0}x_{1}))/\varepsilon_{0}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
With this construction, using the direct computation, we can derive the nonlinear deviation parameter <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Nonlinear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon=\sqrt{1.5}\varepsilon_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mn>1.5</mn></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon=\sqrt{1.5}\varepsilon_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In addition, the linear deviation parameter satisfies <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=0</annotation></semantics></math> and the dimension-penalty term <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{D-d}{D-m}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{D-d}{D-m}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Nonlinear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> therefore yields <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}(\tau)\leq C{\varepsilon_{0}}/{\sqrt{\tau}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>sc</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>τ</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}(\tau)\leq C{\varepsilon_{0}}/{\sqrt{\tau}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4.2 FIR Deviation in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(c) plots the scaled FIR deviation versus <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for fixed noise variance <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each fixed <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, the deviation grows approximately linearly with respect to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, consistent with the bound we derived. The linear trend is most evident for small <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. For larger <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, the deviation values are much smaller, which may make the FIR estimation error more prominent and thus visually weaken the linear pattern.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We extend the FI and FIR analysis to a real-world image dataset using FFHQ with GPE and VAE encodings.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct experiments on the FFHQ dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, which contains <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>70,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> high-quality face images.
Images are downsampled to <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math>, and a diffusion model is trained directly on the pixel data.
For latent-space experiments, we consider both GPE<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> (Geometry Preserving Encoder/Decoder) and VAE models trained on the same FFHQ data. The two models share the same encoder–decoder architecture. We then train diffusion models on the latent representations produced by GPE and VAE, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the diffusion models, we use the official EDM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> training framework with the diffusion transformer (DiT) architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.
The model architecture and training setup are largely consistent between pixel-space and latent-space experiments.
For completeness, Appendix <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H FFHQ Experiments Using the EDM U-Net Architecture ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a> reports results for the experiments in this section and Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ Data with NVAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> using the original EDM U-Net architecture..</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.2 </span>Results</h4>

<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/GPE_FI.png" id="S4.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/287;" width="476" height="287" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/GPE_FIR.png" id="S4.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/289;" width="476" height="289" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>
Values of (a) <math id="S4.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> and (b) <math id="S4.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> plotted versus the noise variance <math id="S4.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, computed from diffusion models trained on different data representations. The <em id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pixel</em> curves correspond to models trained directly on FFHQ images. The <em id="S4.F4.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">latent</em> curves correspond to models trained on latent representations of an image encoder (GPE or VAE) pretrained on FFHQ. We show <math id="S4.F4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\tau}\in[0.01,80]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>τ</mi></msqrt><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.01</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>80</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\tau}\in[0.01,80]</annotation></semantics></math>, excluding smaller <math id="S4.F4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> due to numerical instability.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/EDM_gen_images.png" id="S4.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/GPE_gen_images.png" id="S4.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/VAE_gen_images.png" id="S4.F5.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>
(a) Samples from a diffusion model trained directly on <math id="S4.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math> FFHQ.
(b, c) Samples from latent diffusion models trained on (b) GPE and (c) VAE representations.
Generated latents are mapped back to image space using their respective decoders.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.3.2 Results ‣ 4.3 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows representative generated samples from the diffusion model trained on the pixel space and the latent representations of GPE and VAE.
Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.3.2 Results ‣ 4.3 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> compares the FI and FIR for models trained in pixel space as well as for models trained on GPE and VAE latent representations, while Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4.2 FIR Deviation in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(d) displays the scaled FIR deviation. In computing the scaling factor, we set <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=3\times 64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=3\times 64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math>, the dimension of the pixel space (image dimension), and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=256</annotation></semantics></math>, the latent dimension used for both GPE and VAE.
We observe that the GPE-based curves lie consistently closer to the pixel baseline than those of VAE for both FI and FIR. The better performance of GPE in terms of FI could be explained by Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 3.1 (Fisher Information Bounds for Bi-Lipschitz Encoders). ‣ 3.1 Global Isometry and Fisher Preservation ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. In Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Bi-Lipschitz Constant Estimation for GPE and VAE ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>, we estimate the bi-Lipschitz constants <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> for both encoders. The estimates show that, compared with VAE, the GPE encoder mapping is significantly closer to an isometry: both <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> are close to <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, and the ratio <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/c\approx 1.18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/c\approx 1.18</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas it is substantially larger for the VAE encoder. According to Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 3.1 (Fisher Information Bounds for Bi-Lipschitz Encoders). ‣ 3.1 Global Isometry and Fisher Preservation ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, when <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> are close to <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, the FI in the latent space remains tightly bounded relative to the pixel baseline.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The smaller FIR deviation observed for GPE can be explained by
Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 3.6. ‣ Geometric Interpretation of Encoding Distortion. ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>, which shows that the GPE encoder
satisfies the conditions of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Nonlinear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>. As a result, the deviation bound in
Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Nonlinear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> applies to the GPE latent
representation. This provides a theoretical explanation for the empirical
observation in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4.2 FIR Deviation in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(d), where the
GPE-based model exhibits substantially smaller FIR deviation than the
VAE model, particularly at small noise levels <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ Data with NVAE encoding</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We extend the FI and FIR comparison on the FFHQ dataset to include latent representations produced by NVAE models.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the FFHQ dataset downsampled to <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math> resolution.
For pixel-space experiments, we use the same trained diffusion model as in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>. To study latent-space behavior, we encode the same images using a pretrained NVAE model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.
The NVAE encoder produces latent tensors of size <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20\times d_{\mathbf{z}}\times d_{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\times d_{\mathbf{z}}\times d_{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> at multiple spatial resolutions, and we train separate diffusion models on the latents for each <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
All diffusion models are trained under the EDM framework using the DiT architecture as the score network.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.4.2 </span>Results</h4>

<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F6.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/NVAE_FI.png" id="S4.F6.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/289;" width="476" height="289" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F6.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/NVAE_FIR.png" id="S4.F6.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/289;" width="476" height="289" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>
Values of (a) <math id="S4.F6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> and (b) <math id="S4.F6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> plotted versus the noise variance <math id="S4.F6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> for diffusion models trained on different data representations.
<em id="S4.F6.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Pixel</em> and <em id="S4.F6.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">latent</em> curves denote models trained on FFHQ images and their pretrained NVAE latents, respectively. NVAE was pretrained on FFHQ, with spatial size <math id="S4.F6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20\times d_{\mathbf{z}}\times d_{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\times d_{\mathbf{z}}\times d_{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.F6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> indicated in the legend).
We show <math id="S4.F6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\tau}\in[0.01,80]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>τ</mi></msqrt><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.01</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>80</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\tau}\in[0.01,80]</annotation></semantics></math>, excluding smaller <math id="S4.F6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> due to numerical instability.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.4.2 Results ‣ 4.4 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ Data with NVAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> compares FI and FIR computed using diffusion models trained directly in pixel space versus NVAE latent representations with varying spatial resolutions <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the FI and FIR curves for the latent models move closer to the pixel baseline. This behavior can be explained by the hierarchical structure of NVAE. Latent variables with larger <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> lie closer to the input space and therefore preserve more of the local geometry of the data manifold, resulting in less distortion in the mapping from data space to the latent representation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also observe that for both FI and FIR, the pixel and latent curves remain closely aligned for large <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, while the deviation increases noticeably once <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> becomes smaller. A possible explanation is that when <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is large, the distribution <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is strongly smoothed by Gaussian noise, so the corresponding score functions become dominated by noise and the resulting FIR values carry limited geometric information about the underlying representation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusions</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we introduced a formal information-geometric framework to demystify the “diffusability” of latent spaces in generative modeling. By decomposing denoising complexity—quantified by the rate of change of the MMSE—into Fisher Information (FI) and Fisher Information Rate (FIR), we identified the specific geometric properties required for successful latent diffusion. Our theoretical analysis explicitly decouples the effects of dimensional compression from intrinsic geometric distortion, revealing that latent-space degradation is primarily driven by local curvature and non-linearity injected by the encoder.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, we derived dual requirements for information-preserving encoders: they must be both <em id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">near-isometric</em> to stabilize global FI and <em id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">near-harmonic</em> to minimize the FIR-based geometric complexity penalty. Our experiments on toy Gaussian datasets and high-dimensional FFHQ images validate these metrics as a robust diagnostic suite. We showed that standard VAEs exhibit severe FIR deviations correlated with generation failure, whereas geometry-preserving approaches maintain better spectral regularity. By providing a task-independent way to evaluate latent spaces before training full diffusion models, our framework offers a principled foundation for designing more diffusible autoencoding architectures.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgements</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work was supported in part by the NVIDIA Academic Grants Program, startup funding from The Ohio State University (W. Lee) and NSF award DMS 2427955 (G. Lerman).</p>
</div>
</section>
