<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Supplementary Material</p>
</div>
<div id="p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p3.1" class="ltx_p">The appendices are organized as follows: Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Related Works ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> reviews related literature, while Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Supplementary Derivations and Proofs ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> contains all missing proofs and derivations. Practical estimation of FI and FIR is detailed in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Estimation of FI and FIR ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>, and Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> provides comprehensive experimental setups. Additional mathematical derivations and numerical supplements, relevant to the toy experimental settings, are provided in Appendices <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Gaussian Toy Experiment: Derivations and Additional Results ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> and <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Bi-Lipschitz Constant Estimation for GPE and VAE ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>. Finally, Appendices <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Additional Model Performance Results ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>–<a href="#A9" title="Appendix I Spectra and Connection to Fisher Geometry ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> present further numerical results and spectral analyses, relevant to the real experimental settings.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Related Works</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The term “diffusability” was first introduced by Skorokhodov et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> to describe the extent to which a latent space facilitates the diffusion process. The authors argued that high-frequency components in latent representations reduce diffusability, though they did not provide a rigorous quantitative measure for this effect. Subsequently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> offered further empirical evidence demonstrating that standard representation encoders often induce latent distributions that are inherently difficult for diffusion models to fit.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Several follow-up works have aimed to improve diffusability through specific architectural or training modifications. For instance, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> promoted diffusability by enforcing equivariance in the latent representation, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> targeted the corruption robustness of the latents. Taking a different angle, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> improved diffusability by training the encoder to produce latents that already resemble mildly noised diffusion states, allowing the diffusion prior to denoise from a smoother, better-aligned distribution. Rather than modifying the latent distribution directly, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> proposed denoising latent features early in the pipeline and using them to condition later pixel-space denoising, effectively simplifying the overall problem.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Another distinct line of work focuses on explicitly constraining the latent geometry. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, latent codes are normalized to lie uniformly on a hypersphere. More broadly, alternative geometric priors for autoencoders have gained traction, including the use of hyperbolic latent spaces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">While these prior works have successfully improved diffusability through empirical regularization and structural constraints, they largely lack a unified mathematical framework to quantify why certain representation spaces are easier to model than others. In contrast, our work provides a rigorous geometric analysis of diffusability. By explicitly bounding the Fisher Information Rate (FIR) of the encoded distributions, we establish formal guarantees on how linear encoders and geometric constraints fundamentally alter the stability of the diffusion process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Supplementary Derivations and Proofs</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Proof of Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 2.1 (Hessian representation of FIR). ‣ 2.4 Information-Geometric Formalism ‣ 2 Background ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a></h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fix <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> be the smooth density of the noisy measure <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>. As defined in Section <a href="#S2.SS4" title="2.4 Information-Geometric Formalism ‣ 2 Background ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, the density is given by the convolution of the clean measure <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> with the Gaussian kernel</p>
<table id="A2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\phi_{\tau}(\mathbf{z})=(2\pi\tau)^{-k/2}\exp(-\|\mathbf{z}\|^{2}/(2\tau))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\tau}(\mathbf{z})=(2\pi\tau)^{-k/2}\exp(-\|\mathbf{z}\|^{2}/(2\tau))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">as follows:</p>
<table id="A2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})=(\mu*\phi_{\tau})(\mathbf{x})=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}\phi_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_{0})\,d\mu(\mathbf{x}_{0})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></msub><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})=(\mu*\phi_{\tau})(\mathbf{x})=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}\phi_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_{0})\,d\mu(\mathbf{x}_{0}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">In view of this expression, <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\tau}\in C^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\tau}\in C^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\tau}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\tau}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and all derivatives of <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> decay at least as fast as a Gaussian. We express the Fisher Information (FI) as:</p>
<table id="A2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\|^{2}\,d\mathbf{x}=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}\frac{\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\|^{2}}{p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})}\,d\mathbf{x}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></msub><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\|^{2}\,d\mathbf{x}=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}\frac{\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\|^{2}}{p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})}\,d\mathbf{x}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Using the probabilistic heat equation <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial p_{\tau}}{\partial\tau}=\frac{1}{2}\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial p_{\tau}}{\partial\tau}=\frac{1}{2}\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> from <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Score-based Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we differentiate <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d}{d\tau}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d}{d\tau}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}\left(\frac{2\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}(\partial_{\tau}p_{\tau})}{p_{\tau}}-\frac{\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\|^{2}}{p_{\tau}^{2}}\partial_{\tau}p_{\tau}\right)d\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>τ</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>τ</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}\left(\frac{2\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}(\partial_{\tau}p_{\tau})}{p_{\tau}}-\frac{\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\|^{2}}{p_{\tau}^{2}}\partial_{\tau}p_{\tau}\right)d\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}\left(\frac{\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}(\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau})}{p_{\tau}}-\frac{1}{2}\frac{\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\|^{2}}{p_{\tau}^{2}}\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\right)d\mathbf{x}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}\left(\frac{\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}(\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau})}{p_{\tau}}-\frac{1}{2}\frac{\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\|^{2}}{p_{\tau}^{2}}\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\right)d\mathbf{x}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Integrating the first term by parts (where boundary terms vanish due to Gaussian decay):</p>
<table id="A2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}\frac{\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}(\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau})}{p_{\tau}}\,d\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></msub></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}\frac{\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}(\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau})}{p_{\tau}}\,d\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\,\mathrm{div}\!\left(\frac{\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}}{p_{\tau}}\right)d\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></msub></mstyle><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:1.216em;" width="1.216em"><mi>div</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\,\mathrm{div}\!\left(\frac{\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}}{p_{\tau}}\right)d\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\left(\frac{\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}}{p_{\tau}}-\frac{\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\|^{2}}{p_{\tau}^{2}}\right)d\mathbf{x}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></msub></mstyle><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\left(\frac{\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}}{p_{\tau}}-\frac{\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\|^{2}}{p_{\tau}^{2}}\right)d\mathbf{x}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.6" class="ltx_p">Substituting this back into <a href="#A2.E11" title="In B.1 Proof of Proposition ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Derivations and Proofs ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> yields:</p>
<table id="A2.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{d\tau}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}\left[-\frac{(\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau})^{2}}{p_{\tau}}+\frac{1}{2}\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\frac{\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\|^{2}}{p_{\tau}^{2}}\right]d\mathbf{x}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{d\tau}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}\left[-\frac{(\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau})^{2}}{p_{\tau}}+\frac{1}{2}\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\frac{\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\|^{2}}{p_{\tau}^{2}}\right]d\mathbf{x}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.7" class="ltx_p">To simplify, let <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\log p_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\log p_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}f</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}f\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}f\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Noting that <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}}{p_{\tau}}=\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}+\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}\|^{2}=A+B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}}{p_{\tau}}=\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}+\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}\|^{2}=A+B</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d}{d\tau}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d}{d\tau}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}p_{\tau}\left[-(A+B)^{2}+\frac{1}{2}(A+B)B\right]d\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}p_{\tau}\left[-(A+B)^{2}+\frac{1}{2}(A+B)B\right]d\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}p_{\tau}\left[-A^{2}-\frac{3}{2}AB-\frac{1}{2}B^{2}\right]d\mathbf{x}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}p_{\tau}\left[-A^{2}-\frac{3}{2}AB-\frac{1}{2}B^{2}\right]d\mathbf{x}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.8" class="ltx_p">Now, let <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\tau}=\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\tau}=\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Hessian. The Euclidean Bochner identity for <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\log p_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\log p_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> states:</p>
<table id="A2.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}f\|^{2}=\|\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{x}}f\|_{F}^{2}+\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}f\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}(\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}f)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}f\|^{2}=\|\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{x}}f\|_{F}^{2}+\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}f\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}(\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}f).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.9" class="ltx_p">Integrating <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}f\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}f\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}A</annotation></semantics></math> against the density <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> via integration by parts, and noting that <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{div}(p_{\tau}\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}f)=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}f+p_{\tau}\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}f=p_{\tau}(B+A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>div</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{div}(p_{\tau}\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}f)=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}f+p_{\tau}\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}f=p_{\tau}(B+A)</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int p_{\tau}\frac{1}{2}\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}B\,d\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int p_{\tau}\frac{1}{2}\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}B\,d\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int\frac{1}{2}B\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\,d\mathbf{x}=\int p_{\tau}\left(\frac{1}{2}AB+\frac{1}{2}B^{2}\right)d\mathbf{x}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int\frac{1}{2}B\Delta_{\mathbf{x}}p_{\tau}\,d\mathbf{x}=\int p_{\tau}\left(\frac{1}{2}AB+\frac{1}{2}B^{2}\right)d\mathbf{x},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle-\int p_{\tau}\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}f\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}A\,d\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∫</mo></mstyle><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\int p_{\tau}\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}f\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}A\,d\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int A\,\text{div}(p_{\tau}\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}f)\,d\mathbf{x}=\int p_{\tau}(A^{2}+AB)\,d\mathbf{x}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>div</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int A\,\text{div}(p_{\tau}\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}f)\,d\mathbf{x}=\int p_{\tau}(A^{2}+AB)\,d\mathbf{x}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.10" class="ltx_p">Summing these yields the weighted Bochner identity:</p>
<table id="A2.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}}[\|\mathbf{H}_{\tau}\|_{F}^{2}]=\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}}\left[A^{2}+\frac{3}{2}AB+\frac{1}{2}B^{2}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}}[\|\mathbf{H}_{\tau}\|_{F}^{2}]=\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}}\left[A^{2}+\frac{3}{2}AB+\frac{1}{2}B^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.11" class="ltx_p">Comparing this to <a href="#A2.E14" title="In B.1 Proof of Proposition ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Derivations and Proofs ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, we find:</p>
<table id="A2.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{d}{d\tau}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})=-\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}p_{\tau}\|\mathbf{H}_{\tau}\|_{F}^{2}\,d\mathbf{x}=-\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}}\left[\mathrm{Tr}(\mathbf{H}_{\tau}^{2})\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>Tr</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{d\tau}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})=-\int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}}p_{\tau}\|\mathbf{H}_{\tau}\|_{F}^{2}\,d\mathbf{x}=-\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}}\left[\mathrm{Tr}(\mathbf{H}_{\tau}^{2})\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.12" class="ltx_p">By <a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.4 Information-Geometric Formalism ‣ 2 Background ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})=-\frac{d}{d\tau}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})=-\frac{d}{d\tau}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math>. Substituting the score <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\mathbf{s}_{\tau}=\mathbf{H}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\mathbf{s}_{\tau}=\mathbf{H}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})=\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}}[\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\|_{F}^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})=\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}}[\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\|_{F}^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>, which completes the proof.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Derivation of (<a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>)</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide the detailed derivation for the I-MMSE identity and the Denoising Resistance decomposition. Consider a signal <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> drawn from a distribution <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, and its corruption by additive white Gaussian noise <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{n}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{n}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in the noisy observation:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\tau}=\mathbf{x}+\sqrt{\tau}\mathbf{n}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>τ</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐧</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\tau}=\mathbf{x}+\sqrt{\tau}\mathbf{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is the noise variance. The total variance of the corruption is <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\|\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_{\tau}\|^{2}]=\mathbb{E}[\|-\sqrt{\tau}\mathbf{n}\|^{2}]=\tau k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>τ</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐧</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\|\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_{\tau}\|^{2}]=\mathbb{E}[\|-\sqrt{\tau}\mathbf{n}\|^{2}]=\tau k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})=\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{x}\mid\mathbf{x}_{\tau}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})=\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{x}\mid\mathbf{x}_{\tau}]</annotation></semantics></math> be the MMSE estimator. We can decompose the total noise variance by adding and subtracting this estimator:</p>
<table id="A2.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\|\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_{\tau}\|^{2}]=\mathbb{E}[\|(\mathbf{x}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau}))+(\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})-\mathbf{x}_{\tau})\|^{2}]\\
=\mathbb{E}[\|\mathbf{x}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})\|^{2}]+\mathbb{E}[\|\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})-\mathbf{x}_{\tau}\|^{2}]+2\mathbb{E}[(\mathbf{x}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau}))^{\top}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})-\mathbf{x}_{\tau})]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\|\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_{\tau}\|^{2}]=\mathbb{E}[\|(\mathbf{x}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau}))+(\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})-\mathbf{x}_{\tau})\|^{2}]\\
=\mathbb{E}[\|\mathbf{x}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})\|^{2}]+\mathbb{E}[\|\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})-\mathbf{x}_{\tau}\|^{2}]+2\mathbb{E}[(\mathbf{x}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau}))^{\top}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})-\mathbf{x}_{\tau})].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The cross-term vanishes by the tower property of conditional expectation. Since <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})-\mathbf{x}_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})-\mathbf{x}_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math> is deterministic given <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[(\mathbf{x}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau}))^{\top}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})-\mathbf{x}_{\tau})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[(\mathbf{x}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau}))^{\top}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})-\mathbf{x}_{\tau})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_{\tau}}\left[\mathbb{E}[(\mathbf{x}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau}))^{\top}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})-\mathbf{x}_{\tau})\mid\mathbf{x}_{\tau}]\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_{\tau}}\left[\mathbb{E}[(\mathbf{x}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau}))^{\top}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})-\mathbf{x}_{\tau})\mid\mathbf{x}_{\tau}]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_{\tau}}\left[(\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{x}\mid\mathbf{x}_{\tau}]-\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau}))^{\top}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})-\mathbf{x}_{\tau})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_{\tau}}\left[(\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{x}\mid\mathbf{x}_{\tau}]-\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau}))^{\top}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})-\mathbf{x}_{\tau})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_{\tau}}\left[(\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})-\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau}))^{\top}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})-\mathbf{x}_{\tau})\right]=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_{\tau}}\left[(\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})-\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau}))^{\top}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})-\mathbf{x}_{\tau})\right]=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Next, we invoke Tweedie’s formula <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, which relates the residual of the MMSE estimator to the score function <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{\tau}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{\tau}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A2.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})-\mathbf{x}_{\tau}=\tau\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{\tau}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})=\tau\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})-\mathbf{x}_{\tau}=\tau\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{\tau}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})=\tau\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Substituting (<a href="#A2.E20" title="Equation 20 ‣ B.2 Derivation of () and () ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Derivations and Proofs ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) and the definition <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{MMSE}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})=\mathbb{E}[\|\mathbf{x}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})\|^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>MMSE</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{MMSE}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})=\mathbb{E}[\|\mathbf{x}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})\|^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> into (<a href="#A2.E18" title="Equation 18 ‣ B.2 Derivation of () and () ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Derivations and Proofs ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) yields:</p>
<table id="A2.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau k=\text{MMSE}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})+\tau^{2}\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}}[\|\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})\|^{2}]=\text{MMSE}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})+\tau^{2}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>MMSE</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>MMSE</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau k=\text{MMSE}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})+\tau^{2}\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}}[\|\mathbf{s}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}_{\tau})\|^{2}]=\text{MMSE}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})+\tau^{2}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Rearranging for <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{MMSE}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>MMSE</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{MMSE}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math> directly recovers the I-MMSE identity (<a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) as established in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To derive the Denoising Resistance, we differentiate (<a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) with respect to the noise variance <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> using the product rule:</p>
<table id="A2.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{d\tau}\text{MMSE}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})=\frac{d}{d\tau}\left(k\tau-\tau^{2}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})\right)=k-2\tau\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})-\tau^{2}\frac{d}{d\tau}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mtext>MMSE</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{d\tau}\text{MMSE}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})=\frac{d}{d\tau}\left(k\tau-\tau^{2}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})\right)=k-2\tau\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})-\tau^{2}\frac{d}{d\tau}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Substituting our definition of the Fisher Information Rate (FIR) from (<a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 2.4 Information-Geometric Formalism ‣ 2 Background ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), namely <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})=-\frac{d}{d\tau}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})=-\frac{d}{d\tau}\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain the fundamental decomposition:</p>
<table id="A2.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{d\tau}\text{MMSE}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})=\underbrace{k-2\tau\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})}_{\text{Intrinsic Noise Gain}}+\underbrace{\tau^{2}\mathcal{R}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})}_{\text{Geometric Complexity Penalty}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mtext>MMSE</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Intrinsic Noise Gain</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Geometric Complexity Penalty</mtext></munder></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{d\tau}\text{MMSE}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})=\underbrace{k-2\tau\mathcal{I}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})}_{\text{Intrinsic Noise Gain}}+\underbrace{\tau^{2}\mathcal{R}^{(k)}(\mu_{\tau})}_{\text{Geometric Complexity Penalty}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">As noted in the main text, the <em id="A2.SS2.p3.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Intrinsic Noise Gain</em> represents the baseline linear increase in estimation error due to dimensionality <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, attenuated by the current magnitude of information <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, the <em id="A2.SS2.p3.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Geometric Complexity Penalty</em> represents the additional difficulty imposed by the local curvature of the density. By Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 2.1 (Hessian representation of FIR). ‣ 2.4 Information-Geometric Formalism ‣ 2 Background ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, this term is governed by <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}}[\|\nabla\mathbf{s}_{\tau}\|_{F}^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}}[\|\nabla\mathbf{s}_{\tau}\|_{F}^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that distributions with rapidly changing score functions (high curvature) exhibit a faster degradation in denoising performance as noise scales.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Proof of Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 3.1 (Fisher Information Bounds for Bi-Lipschitz Encoders). ‣ 3.1 Global Isometry and Fisher Preservation ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a></h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we prove that the Fisher Information (FI) in the latent space is bounded by the FI in the data space scaled by the bi-Lipschitz constants of the encoder.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}\subset\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\subset\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the data and latent manifolds, respectively. Let <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathbf{x}}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathbf{x}}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathbf{z}}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathbf{z}}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the tangent spaces at <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})\in\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})\in\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote the manifold score vectors (tangential gradients of the log-density) as:</span></p>
<table id="A2.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\tau}^{(D)}(\mathbf{x})=\nabla_{\mathcal{M}}\log p_{\tau}^{(D)}(\mathbf{x})\in T_{\mathbf{x}}\mathcal{M},\quad\text{and}\quad\mathbf{s}_{\tau}^{(d)}(\mathbf{z})=\nabla_{\mathcal{N}}\log p_{\tau}^{(d)}(\mathbf{z})\in T_{\mathbf{z}}\mathcal{N}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\tau}^{(D)}(\mathbf{x})=\nabla_{\mathcal{M}}\log p_{\tau}^{(D)}(\mathbf{x})\in T_{\mathbf{x}}\mathcal{M},\quad\text{and}\quad\mathbf{s}_{\tau}^{(d)}(\mathbf{z})=\nabla_{\mathcal{N}}\log p_{\tau}^{(d)}(\mathbf{z})\in T_{\mathbf{z}}\mathcal{N}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{E}(\mathbf{x}):T_{\mathbf{x}}\mathcal{M}\to T_{\mathbf{z}}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{E}(\mathbf{x}):T_{\mathbf{x}}\mathcal{M}\to T_{\mathbf{z}}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Jacobian of the encoder restricted to the tangent space. Under the change of variables on manifolds, the densities are related by <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\tau}^{(d)}(\mathbf{z})=p_{\tau}^{(D)}(\mathbf{x})/\sqrt{\det(J_{E}(\mathbf{x})^{\top}J_{E}(\mathbf{x}))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\tau}^{(d)}(\mathbf{z})=p_{\tau}^{(D)}(\mathbf{x})/\sqrt{\det(J_{E}(\mathbf{x})^{\top}J_{E}(\mathbf{x}))}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">As noted in Section <a href="#S2.SS4" title="2.4 Information-Geometric Formalism ‣ 2 Background ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, assuming the log-volume gradient <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathcal{M}}\log\sqrt{\det(J_{E}^{\top}J_{E})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathcal{M}}\log\sqrt{\det(J_{E}^{\top}J_{E})}</annotation></semantics></math> is negligible (a property of near-isometric maps), the chain rule implies the score vectors transform via the adjoint Jacobian operator <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{E}(\mathbf{x})^{\top}:T_{\mathbf{z}}\mathcal{N}\to T_{\mathbf{x}}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{E}(\mathbf{x})^{\top}:T_{\mathbf{z}}\mathcal{N}\to T_{\mathbf{x}}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="A2.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\tau}^{(D)}(\mathbf{x})\approx J_{E}(\mathbf{x})^{\top}\mathbf{s}_{\tau}^{(d)}(\mathbf{z})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\tau}^{(D)}(\mathbf{x})\approx J_{E}(\mathbf{x})^{\top}\mathbf{s}_{\tau}^{(d)}(\mathbf{z}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">The bi-Lipschitz condition on <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> ensures that for any tangent vector <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}\in T_{\mathbf{x}}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}\in T_{\mathbf{x}}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, the singular values of <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{E}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{E}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> are bounded within <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[c,C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[c,C]</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that for the adjoint operator:</span></p>
<table id="A2.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\|\mathbf{s}_{\tau}^{(d)}(\mathbf{z})\|\leq\|J_{E}(\mathbf{x})^{\top}\mathbf{s}_{\tau}^{(d)}(\mathbf{z})\|\leq C\|\mathbf{s}_{\tau}^{(d)}(\mathbf{z})\|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\|\mathbf{s}_{\tau}^{(d)}(\mathbf{z})\|\leq\|J_{E}(\mathbf{x})^{\top}\mathbf{s}_{\tau}^{(d)}(\mathbf{z})\|\leq C\|\mathbf{s}_{\tau}^{(d)}(\mathbf{z})\|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">Substituting (<a href="#A2.E25" title="Equation 25 ‣ Proof. ‣ B.3 Proof of Proposition ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Derivations and Proofs ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>) into this inequality and squaring all terms, we obtain:</span></p>
<table id="A2.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{2}\|\mathbf{s}_{\tau}^{(d)}(\mathbf{z})\|^{2}\leq\|\mathbf{s}_{\tau}^{(D)}(\mathbf{x})\|^{2}\leq C^{2}\|\mathbf{s}_{\tau}^{(d)}(\mathbf{z})\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{2}\|\mathbf{s}_{\tau}^{(d)}(\mathbf{z})\|^{2}\leq\|\mathbf{s}_{\tau}^{(D)}(\mathbf{x})\|^{2}\leq C^{2}\|\mathbf{s}_{\tau}^{(d)}(\mathbf{z})\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">To find the global Fisher Information, we integrate with respect to the probability measure. By the pushforward property of the encoder <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}^{(D)}}[f(E(\mathbf{x}))]=\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}^{(d)}}[f(\mathbf{z})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}^{(D)}}[f(E(\mathbf{x}))]=\mathbb{E}_{p_{\tau}^{(d)}}[f(\mathbf{z})]</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying this to our inequality:</span></p>
<table id="A2.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{2}\mathcal{I}^{(d)}(\mu_{\tau})\leq\mathcal{I}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})\leq C^{2}\mathcal{I}^{(d)}(\mu_{\tau})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{2}\mathcal{I}^{(d)}(\mu_{\tau})\leq\mathcal{I}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})\leq C^{2}\mathcal{I}^{(d)}(\mu_{\tau}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p3.5.1" class="ltx_text">Rearranging these terms to isolate the latent Fisher Information <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math> yields:</span></p>
<table id="A2.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{C^{2}}\mathcal{I}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})\leq\mathcal{I}^{(d)}(\mu_{\tau})\leq\frac{1}{c^{2}}\mathcal{I}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{C^{2}}\mathcal{I}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})\leq\mathcal{I}^{(d)}(\mu_{\tau})\leq\frac{1}{c^{2}}\mathcal{I}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p3.6.1" class="ltx_text">This confirms that if <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\leq 1\leq C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\leq 1\leq C</annotation></semantics></math>, the latent information is ”sandwiched” around the data information.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, we consider the effect on the Intrinsic Noise Gain <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\tau)=k-2\tau\mathcal{I}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\tau)=k-2\tau\mathcal{I}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the latent space, the ambient dimension <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> changes from <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the total shift in the MMSE rate is:</span></p>
<table id="A2.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta G(\tau)=(d-D)-2\tau\left(\mathcal{I}^{(d)}-\mathcal{I}^{(D)}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta G(\tau)=(d-D)-2\tau\left(\mathcal{I}^{(d)}-\mathcal{I}^{(D)}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Under perfect isometry (<math id="A2.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=C=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=C=1</annotation></semantics></math>), we have <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{(d)}=\mathcal{I}^{(D)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{(d)}=\mathcal{I}^{(D)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the degradation is driven solely by the dimensional compression penalty <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta d=D-d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta d=D-d</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Normal-Direction Decomposition for Subspace Measures</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following lemma establishes the “Dimensional Compression Penalty” by showing that normal noise directions contribute a constant, universal term to the FIR. It is used to prove <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 (Linear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS5" title="B.5 Proof of (Linear Stability) ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Derivations and Proofs ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma B.1</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Exact normal-direction contribution)<span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq m\leq D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq m\leq D</annotation></semantics></math> and identify <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{D}\cong\mathbb{R}^{m}\times\mathbb{R}^{D-m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{D}\cong\mathbb{R}^{m}\times\mathbb{R}^{D-m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> be a Borel probability measure on <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, and define a measure
<math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu:=\rho\otimes\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu:=\rho\otimes\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Recalling that <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Gaussian kernel defined in (<a href="#A2.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ B.1 Proof of Proposition ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Derivations and Proofs ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), we note that for every variance <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the smoothed density <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> factorizes as:</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\tau}(y,w)=(\rho*\phi_{\tau}^{(m)})(y)\,\phi_{\tau}^{(D-m)}(w),\qquad(y,w)\in\mathbb{R}^{m}\times\mathbb{R}^{D-m}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\tau}(y,w)=(\rho*\phi_{\tau}^{(m)})(y)\,\phi_{\tau}^{(D-m)}(w),\qquad(y,w)\in\mathbb{R}^{m}\times\mathbb{R}^{D-m},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the Fisher Information Rate satisfies the exact splitting:</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})=\mathcal{R}^{(m)}(\rho_{\tau})+\frac{D-m}{\tau^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})=\mathcal{R}^{(m)}(\rho_{\tau})+\frac{D-m}{\tau^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. By the definition of convolution and the product structure of the standard Gaussian kernel, we have <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\tau}(y,w)=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{m}}\phi_{\tau}^{(m)}(y-y^{\prime})\,\phi_{\tau}^{(D-m)}(w)\,\rho(dy^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\tau}(y,w)=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{m}}\phi_{\tau}^{(m)}(y-y^{\prime})\,\phi_{\tau}^{(D-m)}(w)\,\rho(dy^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. This gives the claimed factorization.
Using <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\mu_{\tau}(y,w)=\log(\rho*\phi_{\tau}^{(m)})(y)+\log\phi_{\tau}^{(D-m)}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\mu_{\tau}(y,w)=\log(\rho*\phi_{\tau}^{(m)})(y)+\log\phi_{\tau}^{(D-m)}(w)</annotation></semantics></math>, the Hessian <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}\log\mu_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}\log\mu_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is block diagonal:</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}_{(y,w)}\log\mu_{\tau}=\text{diag}\left(\nabla_{y}^{2}\log(\rho*\phi_{\tau}^{(m)})(y),\nabla_{w}^{2}\log\phi_{\tau}^{(D-m)}(w)\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>diag</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}_{(y,w)}\log\mu_{\tau}=\text{diag}\left(\nabla_{y}^{2}\log(\rho*\phi_{\tau}^{(m)})(y),\nabla_{w}^{2}\log\phi_{\tau}^{(D-m)}(w)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Consequently, <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla^{2}\log\mu_{\tau}\|_{F}^{2}=\|\nabla_{y}^{2}\log\rho_{\tau}\|_{F}^{2}+\|\nabla_{w}^{2}\log\phi_{\tau}^{(D-m)}\|_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla^{2}\log\mu_{\tau}\|_{F}^{2}=\|\nabla_{y}^{2}\log\rho_{\tau}\|_{F}^{2}+\|\nabla_{w}^{2}\log\phi_{\tau}^{(D-m)}\|_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Applying the identity from <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 2.1 (Hessian representation of FIR). ‣ 2.4 Information-Geometric Formalism ‣ 2 Background ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> and using Fubini’s theorem to integrate over <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{m}\times\mathbb{R}^{D-m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{m}\times\mathbb{R}^{D-m}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})=\mathcal{R}^{(m)}(\rho_{\tau})+\int_{\mathbb{R}^{D-m}}\phi_{\tau}^{(D-m)}(w)\|\nabla_{w}^{2}\log\phi_{\tau}^{(D-m)}(w)\|_{F}^{2}dw." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})=\mathcal{R}^{(m)}(\rho_{\tau})+\int_{\mathbb{R}^{D-m}}\phi_{\tau}^{(D-m)}(w)\|\nabla_{w}^{2}\log\phi_{\tau}^{(D-m)}(w)\|_{F}^{2}dw.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{w}^{2}\log\phi_{\tau}^{(D-m)}(w)=-\frac{1}{\tau}I_{D-m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>τ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{w}^{2}\log\phi_{\tau}^{(D-m)}(w)=-\frac{1}{\tau}I_{D-m}</annotation></semantics></math>, the second term evaluates exactly to <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{D-m}{\tau^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{D-m}{\tau^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, noting <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\phi_{\tau}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\phi_{\tau}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.5 </span>Proof of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 (Linear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> (Linear Stability)</h3>

<div id="A2.SS5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma B.1 (Exact normal-direction contribution). ‣ B.4 Normal-Direction Decomposition for Subspace Measures ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Derivations and Proofs ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>, <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})=\mathcal{R}^{(m)}(\rho_{\tau})+\frac{D-m}{\tau^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})=\mathcal{R}^{(m)}(\rho_{\tau})+\frac{D-m}{\tau^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is supported on an <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-plane in <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> with tangential coordinate law <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{\#}\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{\#}\rho</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(d)}(\mu_{Z,\tau})=\mathcal{R}^{(m)}((\mathbf{A}_{\#}\rho)_{\tau})+\frac{d-m}{\tau^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(d)}(\mu_{Z,\tau})=\mathcal{R}^{(m)}((\mathbf{A}_{\#}\rho)_{\tau})+\frac{d-m}{\tau^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">The difference is:</span></p>
<table id="A2.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})-\mathcal{R}^{(d)}(\mu_{Z,\tau})=\left(\mathcal{R}^{(m)}(\rho_{\tau})-\mathcal{R}^{(m)}((\mathbf{A}_{\#}\rho)_{\tau})\right)+\frac{D-d}{\tau^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})-\mathcal{R}^{(d)}(\mu_{Z,\tau})=\left(\mathcal{R}^{(m)}(\rho_{\tau})-\mathcal{R}^{(m)}((\mathbf{A}_{\#}\rho)_{\tau})\right)+\frac{D-d}{\tau^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS5.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS5.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text">To bound the tangential distortion, let <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=\mathbf{A}_{\#}\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=\mathbf{A}_{\#}\rho</annotation></semantics></math>. Using polar decomposition <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}=\mathbf{R}\mathbf{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐑𝐒</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}=\mathbf{R}\mathbf{S}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is orthogonal and <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}=(\mathbf{A}^{\top}\mathbf{A})^{1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐒</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐀</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}=(\mathbf{A}^{\top}\mathbf{A})^{1/2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we note that <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves FIR. Under Assumption (NI<sub id="A2.SS5.p1.3.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A2.SS5.p1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A</span></sub>), the eigenvalues of <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐒</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}</annotation></semantics></math> lie in <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sqrt{1-\delta},\sqrt{1+\delta}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sqrt{1-\delta},\sqrt{1+\delta}]</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{S}\|_{2}\leq\sqrt{1+\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐒</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{S}\|_{2}\leq\sqrt{1+\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{S}^{-1}\|_{2}\leq 1/\sqrt{1-\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{S}^{-1}\|_{2}\leq 1/\sqrt{1-\delta}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We use the elementary bound <math id="A2.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{M}\mathbf{H}\|_{F}\leq\|\mathbf{M}\|_{2}\|\mathbf{H}\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐌𝐇</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{M}\mathbf{H}\|_{F}\leq\|\mathbf{M}\|_{2}\|\mathbf{H}\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="A2.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\times m</annotation></semantics></math> matrix <math id="A2.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, this gives:</span></p>
<table id="A2.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{S}^{-\top}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{S}^{-1}\|_{F}\leq\|\mathbf{S}^{-1}\|_{2}^{2}\,\|\mathbf{H}\|_{F},\qquad\|\mathbf{S}^{-\top}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{S}^{-1}\|_{F}\geq\|\mathbf{S}\|_{2}^{-2}\,\|\mathbf{H}\|_{F}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⊤</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐇𝐒</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⊤</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐇𝐒</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐒</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{S}^{-\top}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{S}^{-1}\|_{F}\leq\|\mathbf{S}^{-1}\|_{2}^{2}\,\|\mathbf{H}\|_{F},\qquad\|\mathbf{S}^{-\top}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{S}^{-1}\|_{F}\geq\|\mathbf{S}\|_{2}^{-2}\,\|\mathbf{H}\|_{F}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS5.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">By coordinate transformation and the chain rule:</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{y}}^{2}\log\nu_{\tau}(\mathbf{y})=\mathbf{S}^{-\top}\bigl(\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}^{2}\log\widetilde{\nu}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\bigr)\,\mathbf{S}^{-1}\Big|_{\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{S}^{-1}\mathbf{y}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⊤</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{y}}^{2}\log\nu_{\tau}(\mathbf{y})=\mathbf{S}^{-\top}\bigl(\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}^{2}\log\widetilde{\nu}_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\bigr)\,\mathbf{S}^{-1}\Big|_{\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{S}^{-1}\mathbf{y}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS5.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS5.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">Using the operator norm bounds from (<a href="#A2.E32" title="Equation 32 ‣ Proof. ‣ B.5 Proof of (Linear Stability) ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Derivations and Proofs ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>), we obtain <math id="A2.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+\delta)^{-(\tfrac{m}{2}+2)}\,\mathcal{R}^{(m)}(\rho_{\tau})\leq\mathcal{R}^{(m)}(\nu_{\tau})\leq(1+\delta)^{\tfrac{m}{2}+2}\,\mathcal{R}^{(m)}(\rho_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+\delta)^{-(\tfrac{m}{2}+2)}\,\mathcal{R}^{(m)}(\rho_{\tau})\leq\mathcal{R}^{(m)}(\nu_{\tau})\leq(1+\delta)^{\tfrac{m}{2}+2}\,\mathcal{R}^{(m)}(\rho_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Applying a Taylor expansion for <math id="A2.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we find <math id="A2.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{R}^{(m)}(\nu_{\tau})-\mathcal{R}^{(m)}(\rho_{\tau})|\leq C\,\delta\,\mathcal{R}^{(m)}(\rho_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{R}^{(m)}(\nu_{\tau})-\mathcal{R}^{(m)}(\rho_{\tau})|\leq C\,\delta\,\mathcal{R}^{(m)}(\rho_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math>. Substituting this back into (<a href="#A2.E31" title="Equation 31 ‣ Proof. ‣ B.5 Proof of (Linear Stability) ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Derivations and Proofs ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>) and dividing by <math id="A2.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(d)}(\mu_{Z,\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(d)}(\mu_{Z,\tau})</annotation></semantics></math> yields the stated bounds for <math id="A2.SS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>sc</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.6 </span>Proof of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Nonlinear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> (Nonlinear Stability)</h3>

<div id="A2.SS6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix <math id="A2.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="A2.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=(E_{\mathrm{lin}}^{x_{0}})_{\#}\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>lin</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=(E_{\mathrm{lin}}^{x_{0}})_{\#}\mu</annotation></semantics></math>. The bounded Hessian <math id="A2.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla^{2}E\|\leq L_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla^{2}E\|\leq L_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="A2.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|E(x)-E_{\mathrm{lin}}^{x_{0}}(x)\|^{2}\leq\varepsilon^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>lin</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|E(x)-E_{\mathrm{lin}}^{x_{0}}(x)\|^{2}\leq\varepsilon^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (integrated over <math id="A2.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>), thus <math id="A2.SS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}(\mu_{Z},\nu)\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}(\mu_{Z},\nu)\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="A2.SS6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta,\tilde{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta,\tilde{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> be measures on <math id="A2.SS6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. We expand the FIR difference:</span></p>
<table id="A2.SS6.p1.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.SS6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(d)}(\eta_{\tau})-\mathcal{R}^{(d)}(\tilde{\eta}_{\tau})=\int(\eta_{\tau}-\tilde{\eta}_{\tau})\|\nabla^{2}\log\eta_{\tau}\|_{F}^{2}dx+\\
\int\tilde{\eta}_{\tau}(\|\nabla^{2}\log\eta_{\tau}\|_{F}^{2}-\|\nabla^{2}\log\tilde{\eta}_{\tau}\|_{F}^{2})dx." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">d</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">d</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(d)}(\eta_{\tau})-\mathcal{R}^{(d)}(\tilde{\eta}_{\tau})=\int(\eta_{\tau}-\tilde{\eta}_{\tau})\|\nabla^{2}\log\eta_{\tau}\|_{F}^{2}dx+\\
\int\tilde{\eta}_{\tau}(\|\nabla^{2}\log\eta_{\tau}\|_{F}^{2}-\|\nabla^{2}\log\tilde{\eta}_{\tau}\|_{F}^{2})dx.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS6.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS6.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text">Using the kernel bounds <math id="A2.SS6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla^{2}\log\eta_{\tau}\|_{F}\leq C/\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla^{2}\log\eta_{\tau}\|_{F}\leq C/\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS6.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\eta_{\tau}-\tilde{\eta}_{\tau}\|_{L^{1}}\leq C\tau^{-1/2}W_{2}(\eta,\tilde{\eta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\eta_{\tau}-\tilde{\eta}_{\tau}\|_{L^{1}}\leq C\tau^{-1/2}W_{2}(\eta,\tilde{\eta})</annotation></semantics></math>, we bound the first term by <math id="A2.SS6.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="C\tau^{-5/2}W_{2}(\eta,\tilde{\eta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\tau^{-5/2}W_{2}(\eta,\tilde{\eta})</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, using the Frobenius difference identity <math id="A2.SS6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A\|^{2}-\|B\|^{2}=(A-B):(A+B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A\|^{2}-\|B\|^{2}=(A-B):(A+B)</annotation></semantics></math>, the second term is also bounded by <math id="A2.SS6.p1.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="C\tau^{-5/2}W_{2}(\eta,\tilde{\eta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\tau^{-5/2}W_{2}(\eta,\tilde{\eta})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Applying this to <math id="A2.SS6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=\mu_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=\mu_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\eta}=\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\eta}=\nu</annotation></semantics></math> gives <math id="A2.SS6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{R}^{(d)}(\mu_{Z,\tau})-\mathcal{R}^{(d)}(\nu_{\tau})|\leq\frac{C\varepsilon}{\tau^{5/2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{R}^{(d)}(\mu_{Z,\tau})-\mathcal{R}^{(d)}(\nu_{\tau})|\leq\frac{C\varepsilon}{\tau^{5/2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Combined with the linear results, this establishes the nonlinear stability bounds.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.7 </span>Proof of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 3.6. ‣ Geometric Interpretation of Encoding Distortion. ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a> (Application to GPE)</h3>

<div id="A2.SS7.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, an encoder minimizing the GPE loss satisfies a weak <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-bi-Lipschitz property on a set <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subset M\times M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subset M\times M</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu\times\mu)(B)\leq\epsilon_{GPE}^{1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu\times\mu)(B)\leq\epsilon_{GPE}^{1/2}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=A^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=A^{c}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\mathrm{fib}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>fib</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\mathrm{fib}}</annotation></semantics></math> be points with ”good fibers” such that <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(B_{y})\leq\epsilon_{GPE}^{1/4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(B_{y})\leq\epsilon_{GPE}^{1/4}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Chebyshev, <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(M\setminus\Omega_{\mathrm{fib}})\leq\epsilon_{GPE}^{1/4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>fib</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(M\setminus\Omega_{\mathrm{fib}})\leq\epsilon_{GPE}^{1/4}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, the local distortion <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(x)=\int_{\mathbb{S}^{m-1}}(\|dE_{x}(v)\|^{2}-1)^{2}dv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(x)=\int_{\mathbb{S}^{m-1}}(\|dE_{x}(v)\|^{2}-1)^{2}dv</annotation></semantics></math> integrated over the sphere yields <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(0)\geq\frac{2\omega_{m}}{m(m+2)}\|\mathbf{A}_{0}^{\top}\mathbf{A}_{0}-\mathbf{I}_{m}\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(0)\geq\frac{2\omega_{m}}{m(m+2)}\|\mathbf{A}_{0}^{\top}\mathbf{A}_{0}-\mathbf{I}_{m}\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Inversion gives <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\propto\epsilon_{GPE}^{1/(4(m+5))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∝</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\propto\epsilon_{GPE}^{1/(4(m+5))}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="A2.SS7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, the remainder <math id="A2.SS7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(x)=\|E(x)-E(0)-\nabla E(0)x\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(x)=\|E(x)-E(0)-\nabla E(0)x\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is analyzed in <math id="A2.SS7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{1}(M,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{1}(M,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math>. Taylor’s theorem provides <math id="A2.SS7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(x)\leq\frac{L_{E}^{2}}{4}\|x\|^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(x)\leq\frac{L_{E}^{2}}{4}\|x\|^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Integrating over <math id="A2.SS7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> split into <math id="A2.SS7.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (good slice) and <math id="A2.SS7.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (bad slice):</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{B_{0}}R(x)d\mu(x)\leq\frac{L_{E}^{2}D_{M}^{4}}{4}\mu(B_{0})\leq\frac{L_{E}^{2}D_{M}^{4}}{4}\epsilon_{GPE}^{1/4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>M</mi><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>M</mi><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{B_{0}}R(x)d\mu(x)\leq\frac{L_{E}^{2}D_{M}^{4}}{4}\mu(B_{0})\leq\frac{L_{E}^{2}D_{M}^{4}}{4}\epsilon_{GPE}^{1/4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS7.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS7.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="A2.SS7.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use the spatial expansion <math id="A2.SS7.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(x)\leq 2(e^{\epsilon_{GPE}^{1/4}}-1)+2(e^{\epsilon_{GPE}^{1/4}}+1+\delta)M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msubsup></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msubsup></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(x)\leq 2(e^{\epsilon_{GPE}^{1/4}}-1)+2(e^{\epsilon_{GPE}^{1/4}}+1+\delta)M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Combining these gives the bound for <math id="A2.SS7.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> as derived in the main text.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Estimation of FI and FIR</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we describe how the Fisher Information (FI) and Fisher Information Rate (FIR) are computed in our experiments based on trained diffusion models. We specifically focus on the computational strategies that allow these metrics to scale to high-dimensional neural architectures.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fisher Information (FI).</h5>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The FI can be expressed using the score function <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>. For a given noise variance <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and dimension <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, the FI is calculated as:</p>
<table id="A3.E33" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A3.E33X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E33X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{I}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{I}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E33X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{D}}p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\,\bigl\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\bigr\|^{2}\,d\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝐝</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{D}}p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\,\bigl\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\bigr\|^{2}\,d\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.E33Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E33Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}\sim p_{\tau}}\bigl[\|s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\|^{2}\bigr]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}\sim p_{\tau}}\bigl[\|s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\|^{2}\bigr]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.E33Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E33Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\bigl\|s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}^{(n)})\bigr\|^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\bigl\|s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}^{(n)})\bigr\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{(n)}</annotation></semantics></math> are samples drawn from the smoothed distribution <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>. In practice, these are obtained by adding Gaussian noise <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\tau\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\tau\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math> to clean data samples from the empirical distribution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fisher Information Rate (FIR).</h5>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The FIR is computed as the dissipation rate of the FI. Using the Hessian representation from Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 2.1 (Hessian representation of FIR). ‣ 2.4 Information-Geometric Formalism ‣ 2 Background ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, we have:</p>
<table id="A3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{R}^{(D)}_{\tau}(\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{R}^{(D)}_{\tau}(\mu)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\frac{d}{d\tau}\,\mathcal{I}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\frac{d}{d\tau}\,\mathcal{I}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}\sim p_{\tau}}\Big[\mathrm{tr}\Big(\big(\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\big)^{2}\Big)\Big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}\sim p_{\tau}}\Big[\mathrm{tr}\Big(\big(\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\big)^{2}\Big)\Big]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\mathrm{tr}\Big(\big(\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}^{(n)})\big)^{2}\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\mathrm{tr}\Big(\big(\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}^{(n)})\big)^{2}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For high-dimensional neural networks, explicitly forming the <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\times D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\times D</annotation></semantics></math> score Jacobian <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}s_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}s_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is computationally intractable, requiring <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(D^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(D^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> memory and <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(D^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(D^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> operations for the matrix square. We circumvent this by employing the <span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hutchinson trace estimator</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Crucially, because the score is a conservative vector field, its Jacobian is a symmetric Hessian matrix (<math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}s_{\tau}=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}^{2}\log p_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}s_{\tau}=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}^{2}\log p_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>). Therefore, the trace of its square equates to its squared Frobenius norm, which can be written as an expectation over probe vectors <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{tr}\!\big((\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}))^{2}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.670em;" width="0.670em"><mi>tr</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{tr}\!\big((\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}))^{2}\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{v}}\!\left[\big\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\,\mathbf{v}\big\|^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{v}}\!\left[\big\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\,\mathbf{v}\big\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx\frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\big\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\,\mathbf{v}^{(m)}\big\|^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx\frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\big\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\,\mathbf{v}^{(m)}\big\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the probe vectors <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}^{(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}^{(m)}</annotation></semantics></math> are drawn independently from a Rademacher distribution (entries in <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{-1,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{-1,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>). This leads to the empirical estimator:</p>
<table id="A3.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(D)}_{\tau}(\mu)\approx\frac{1}{NM}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\big\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}^{(n)})\,\mathbf{v}^{(m)}\big\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(D)}_{\tau}(\mu)\approx\frac{1}{NM}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\big\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}^{(n)})\,\mathbf{v}^{(m)}\big\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implementation via Directional Derivatives.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The term <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Jacobian-vector product (JVP)</span>, representing the directional derivative of the score function. In modern auto-differentiation frameworks (e.g., <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink ltx_path ltx_font_typewriter ltx_ref_self">torch.autograd.functional.jvp</span> in PyTorch), this is evaluated in <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(D)</annotation></semantics></math> time—comparable to a single forward pass—without ever explicitly computing or storing the full Jacobian matrix.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Alternatively, to avoid second-order automatic differentiation entirely or further reduce memory overhead, the term can be approximated via a first-order finite difference. For a small step size <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\,\mathbf{v}\approx\frac{s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}+\varepsilon\mathbf{v})-s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})}{\varepsilon}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>ε</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\,\mathbf{v}\approx\frac{s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}+\varepsilon\mathbf{v})-s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})}{\varepsilon}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This yields the gradient-free estimator:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(D)}_{\tau}(\mu)\approx\frac{1}{NM}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\left\|\frac{s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}^{(n)}+\varepsilon\mathbf{v}^{(m)})-s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}^{(n)})}{\varepsilon}\right\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>ε</mi></mfrac><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(D)}_{\tau}(\mu)\approx\frac{1}{NM}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\left\|\frac{s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}^{(n)}+\varepsilon\mathbf{v}^{(m)})-s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x}^{(n)})}{\varepsilon}\right\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, the JVP approach was found to be highly accurate and numerically stable, providing a scalable means to monitor the geometric complexity penalty during the diffusion process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Score Function in Each Experiment.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, the score function is obtained directly from the trained diffusion model.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the experiments described in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 FI and FIR Comparison in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>
and Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 FIR Deviation in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>,
we use a noise-prediction diffusion model.
The empirical score is approximated using the model’s predicted noise
<math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}(\mathbf{x},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}(\mathbf{x},t)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\approx-\frac{1}{\sqrt{\tau(t)}}\,\epsilon_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\approx-\frac{1}{\sqrt{\tau(t)}}\,\epsilon_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by the forward noising schedule.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the experiments described in Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ Data with NVAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>
and Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>,
we use a denoiser parameterization.
In this case, the score is approximated as</p>
<table id="A3.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\approx-\frac{\mathbf{x}-D_{\theta}(\mathbf{x},\tau)}{\tau}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>τ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{\tau}(\mathbf{x})\approx-\frac{\mathbf{x}-D_{\theta}(\mathbf{x},\tau)}{\tau},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\theta}(\mathbf{x},\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\theta}(\mathbf{x},\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the trained denoiser.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In all experiments, FI and FIR are computed using
equation <a href="#A3.E33" title="Equation 33 ‣ Fisher Information (FI). ‣ Appendix C Estimation of FI and FIR ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>
and equation <a href="#A3.E34" title="Equation 34 ‣ Fisher Information Rate (FIR). ‣ Appendix C Estimation of FI and FIR ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>.
All required gradients are evaluated via automatic differentiation.
For estimation of the values, we use <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1000</annotation></semantics></math> samples and <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=50</annotation></semantics></math> Hutchinson probe vectors.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In all experiments, FI and FIR are computed using
equation <a href="#A3.E33" title="Equation 33 ‣ Fisher Information (FI). ‣ Appendix C Estimation of FI and FIR ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>
and equation <a href="#A3.E34" title="Equation 34 ‣ Fisher Information Rate (FIR). ‣ Appendix C Estimation of FI and FIR ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>.
All required gradients are evaluated via automatic differentiation.
For diffusion models with the EDM framework and DiT architecture trained directly on FFHQ images (Sections <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ Data with NVAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> and <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>) and on GPE latent representations (Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>), we use <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=200</annotation></semantics></math> samples and <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=20</annotation></semantics></math> Hutchinson probe vectors due to the larger model size.
For all other experiments, we use <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1000</annotation></semantics></math> samples and <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=50</annotation></semantics></math> Hutchinson probe vectors.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Experimental Details</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide the model architectures, training configurations, and sampling parameters used in our experiments.
Each subsection corresponds to the experimental setting described in the main text.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>FI and FIR Comparison in the Gaussian Toy Setting</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the experiment in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 FI and FIR Comparison in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we first describe the architecture of the tiny diffusion model used in this setting.
The denoising network in the tiny diffusion model is implemented as an MLP (multilayer perceptron). We use a hidden dimension of <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> hidden layers, and apply sinusoidal embeddings of dimension <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math> to both the input data and the diffusion time.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For all experiments, we use <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> training samples with batch size <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>256</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256</annotation></semantics></math> and train for <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> epochs. The diffusion process uses <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> timesteps with a linear noise <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-schedule. We use the AdamW optimizer with a learning rate of <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>FIR Deviation in the Gaussian Toy Setting</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection provides the experimental details for the study in
Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 FIR Deviation in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>. The model and training configuration follows
the Gaussian toy experiment in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 FI and FIR Comparison in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The only modification concerns the input embedding used in the tiny diffusion model.
In Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 FI and FIR Comparison in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, sinusoidal embeddings were applied to the input
coordinates. Since the experiments in this section consider data whose input
dimension may exceed two, we instead use the identity embedding for all tiny
diffusion models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE Encoding</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection provides the experimental details for the study described in
Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">GPE and VAE</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The GPE and VAE models employ the same fully convolutional encoder and decoder
architectures and differ only in their training objectives, resulting in
different latent representations while keeping the network architecture identical.
We adopt the architecture configuration provided for the CelebA dataset at
<math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math> resolution in the official implementation<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              <a href="https://github.com/wonjunee/GPE_codes" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/wonjunee/GPE_codes</a></span></span></span>.
The latent dimension is set to <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>256</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Both models are trained on FFHQ images at <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math> resolution using a batch size of <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> and learning rate <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For GPE, training is performed in stages: the encoder is first trained for <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> hours, followed by training the decoder for <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> hours while keeping the encoder fixed.
To improve robustness, the decoder is further trained for an additional <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> hours by adding Gaussian noise with standard deviation <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05</annotation></semantics></math> to the encoder outputs before feeding them into the decoder.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the VAE model, we employ a <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-annealing strategy, gradually increasing the KL-divergence weight <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> during training.
Specifically, we begin with a warm-up phase using <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\{0,10^{-3},10^{-2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\{0,10^{-3},10^{-2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, training each stage for <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> hours, followed by training with <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> hours.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Diffusion models</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the pixel-space experiment, we train a diffusion model directly on FFHQ images at <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math> resolution.
For the latent-space experiments, the trained GPE and VAE encoders produce latent vectors of dimension <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>256</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256</annotation></semantics></math>.
These vectors are reshaped into <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16\times 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times 16</annotation></semantics></math> tensors using a serpentine ordering and normalized using Gaussian statistics prior to diffusion training. Diffusion models are then trained separately on the GPE and VAE latent representations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The diffusion model architecture and training setup are largely consistent between the pixel-space and latent-space experiments.
We use a DiT backbone with hidden size <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="768" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>768</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">768</annotation></semantics></math>, depth <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> attention heads as the score network, following the official implementation<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              <a href="https://github.com/facebookresearch/DiT" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/facebookresearch/DiT</a></span></span></span>.
The patch size is adjusted according to the input representation: since the pixel-space distribution and the GPE latent distribution benefit from finer spatial resolution for stable training, we use patch size <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> for the pixel-space model, patch size <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> for the GPE latent model, and patch size <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> for the VAE latent model.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">All diffusion models are trained within the EDM framework in the unconditional setting using the official implementation<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              <a href="https://github.com/NVlabs/edm" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/NVlabs/edm</a></span></span></span>.
We use a batch size of 256 and dropout rate <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05</annotation></semantics></math>, with no data augmentation.
Optimization is performed using Adam with <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}=0.999" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.999</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}=0.999</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=10^{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=10^{-8}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the pixel-space and GPE latent experiments, training is more computationally demanding and exhibits slower convergence.
Therefore, we use the same training configuration for both settings: batch size <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64</annotation></semantics></math> and a cosine learning-rate schedule with a <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> kimg warmup, maximum learning rate <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, minimum learning rate <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-7}</annotation></semantics></math>, and cycle length <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> kimg.
The pixel-space model is trained for <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> kimg, the GPE latent model for <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> kimg, and the VAE latent model for <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> kimg, where “kimg” denotes thousands of images processed during training.
For the VAE latent experiments, we use patch size <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>256</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256</annotation></semantics></math>, and a learning-rate schedule consisting of a <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> kimg warmup followed by a constant learning rate of <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For sampling of the diffusion model, we follow the standard EDM procedure with <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math> sampling steps, <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\min}=0.002" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.002</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\min}=0.002</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}=80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>80</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}=80</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{churn}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>churn</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{churn}}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.4 </span>FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ Data with NVAE Encoding</h3>

<div id="A4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection provides the experimental details for the study in
Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ Data with NVAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">NVAE</h5>

<div id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To obtain latent representations of the FFHQ dataset, we use a publicly available pretrained NVAE model trained on FFHQ.<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              We use the publicly available pretrained NVAE model: <a href="https://github.com/NVlabs/NVAE" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/NVlabs/NVAE</a>.</span></span></span>
NVAE is a hierarchical variational autoencoder whose encoder produces multiple latent tensors at different spatial resolutions.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To maintain consistency with the pixel-space experiments, we use <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math> resolution images as input to the encoder. Although the pretrained NVAE model was originally trained on <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math> images, the encoder is fully convolutional and can therefore process inputs of different spatial resolutions without modifying the architecture.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Diffusion models</h5>

<div id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the pixel-space experiments, we use the same diffusion model trained on FFHQ data as described in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the latent-space experiments, the NVAE encoder produces latent tensors of size <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20\times d_{\mathbf{z}}\times d_{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\times d_{\mathbf{z}}\times d_{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> at spatial resolutions <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{z}}\in\{32,16,8,4,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{z}}\in\{32,16,8,4,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the channel dimension. Since multiple latent tensors exist at the same resolution level, we select a single representative latent for each <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, choosing the one closest to the input space in the encoder hierarchy. Diffusion models are then trained separately on the latent representations corresponding to each <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the latent-space experiments, the patch size of the diffusion models is adjusted according to the input spatial resolution.
We use patch size <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{z}}=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{z}}=32</annotation></semantics></math>, patch size <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{z}}\in\{16,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{z}}\in\{16,8\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and patch size <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{z}}\in\{4,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{z}}\in\{4,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The learning rate is linearly warmed up for <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> kimg and then kept constant at <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each model is trained for <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> kimg.
All other model architecture and training parameters follow those in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Gaussian Toy Experiment: Derivations and Additional Results</h2>

<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Derivation Relevant for the Experiment in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 FI and FIR Comparison in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a></h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide derivations for the experiment in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 FI and FIR Comparison in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.
We consider samples <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and construct encoded representations <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, where the encoder <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is applied pointwise.
For <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider functions given by common activation functions, including ReLU, Leaky ReLU (with negative slope <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>), GELU, sigmoid, and tanh.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We derive the Fisher information bounds in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 3.1 (Fisher Information Bounds for Bi-Lipschitz Encoders). ‣ 3.1 Global Isometry and Fisher Preservation ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and analyze the curvature of the encoder used in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Fisher Information Rate and Second-Order Distortion ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, relating to the discussion there on encoder curvature injection into the FIR.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">ReLU encoder</h5>

<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the pointwise ReLU encoder</p>
<table id="A5.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\mathbf{x})=\mathrm{ReLU}(\mathbf{x})=(\max\{x_{1},0\},\max\{x_{2},0\})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ReLU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\mathbf{x})=\mathrm{ReLU}(\mathbf{x})=(\max\{x_{1},0\},\max\{x_{2},0\}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We derive the FI bound in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 3.1 (Fisher Information Bounds for Bi-Lipschitz Encoders). ‣ 3.1 Global Isometry and Fisher Preservation ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
Consider the pointwise ReLU encoder. Let <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(x_{1},x_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(x_{1},x_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="A5.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\mathbf{x})=\mathrm{ReLU}(\mathbf{x})=(\max\{x_{1},0\},\max\{x_{2},0\})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ReLU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\mathbf{x})=\mathrm{ReLU}(\mathbf{x})=(\max\{x_{1},0\},\max\{x_{2},0\}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Its Jacobian is</p>
<table id="A5.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{E}(\mathbf{x})=\begin{pmatrix}\mathbf{1}_{x_{1}&gt;0}&amp;0\\
0&amp;\mathbf{1}_{x_{2}&gt;0}\end{pmatrix}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{E}(\mathbf{x})=\begin{pmatrix}\mathbf{1}_{x_{1}&gt;0}&amp;0\\
0&amp;\mathbf{1}_{x_{2}&gt;0}\end{pmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">away from the nondifferentiable set <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{x_{1}=0\}\cup\{x_{2}=0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{1}=0\}\cup\{x_{2}=0\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any tangent vector <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}=(v_{1},v_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}=(v_{1},v_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A5.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|J_{E}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{v}\|^{2}=\mathbf{1}_{x_{1}&gt;0}v_{1}^{2}+\mathbf{1}_{x_{2}&gt;0}v_{2}^{2}\leq\|\mathbf{v}\|^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|J_{E}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{v}\|^{2}=\mathbf{1}_{x_{1}&gt;0}v_{1}^{2}+\mathbf{1}_{x_{2}&gt;0}v_{2}^{2}\leq\|\mathbf{v}\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">so the upper Lipschitz constant is <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=1</annotation></semantics></math> and lower constant is <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Substituting these constants into Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 3.1 (Fisher Information Bounds for Bi-Lipschitz Encoders). ‣ 3.1 Global Isometry and Fisher Preservation ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> yields the bound</p>
<table id="A5.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{(D)}\leq\mathcal{I}^{(d)}\leq\infty." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{(D)}\leq\mathcal{I}^{(d)}\leq\infty.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, for this ReLU encoder <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\mathbf{x})=(E_{1}(\mathbf{x}),E_{2}(\mathbf{x}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\mathbf{x})=(E_{1}(\mathbf{x}),E_{2}(\mathbf{x}))</annotation></semantics></math>, the Hessians satisfy</p>
<table id="A5.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}^{2}E_{1}(\mathbf{x})=\begin{pmatrix}\delta(x_{1})&amp;0\\
0&amp;0\end{pmatrix},\qquad\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}^{2}E_{2}(\mathbf{x})=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;0\\
0&amp;\delta(x_{2})\end{pmatrix}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}^{2}E_{1}(\mathbf{x})=\begin{pmatrix}\delta(x_{1})&amp;0\\
0&amp;0\end{pmatrix},\qquad\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}^{2}E_{2}(\mathbf{x})=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;0\\
0&amp;\delta(x_{2})\end{pmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Dirac delta distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Thus the encoder curvature vanishes for <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{2}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{2}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, since ReLU is piecewise linear. At <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the second derivatives become singular through the Dirac delta terms, introducing concentrated curvature.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Leaky ReLU encoder</h5>

<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next we consider the pointwise Leaky ReLU encoder with negative slope <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A5.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\mathbf{x})=\mathrm{LeakyReLU}_{\alpha}(\mathbf{x})=(\phi_{\alpha}(x_{1}),\phi_{\alpha}(x_{2})),\quad\phi_{\alpha}(x)=\begin{cases}x,&amp;x&gt;0,\\
\alpha x,&amp;x\leq 0.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>LeakyReLU</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\mathbf{x})=\mathrm{LeakyReLU}_{\alpha}(\mathbf{x})=(\phi_{\alpha}(x_{1}),\phi_{\alpha}(x_{2})),\quad\phi_{\alpha}(x)=\begin{cases}x,&amp;x&gt;0,\\
\alpha x,&amp;x\leq 0.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Its Jacobian is</p>
<table id="A5.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{E}(\mathbf{x})=\begin{pmatrix}\phi_{\alpha}^{\prime}(x_{1})&amp;0\\
0&amp;\phi_{\alpha}^{\prime}(x_{2})\end{pmatrix},\qquad\phi_{\alpha}^{\prime}(x)\in\{\alpha,1\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{E}(\mathbf{x})=\begin{pmatrix}\phi_{\alpha}^{\prime}(x_{1})&amp;0\\
0&amp;\phi_{\alpha}^{\prime}(x_{2})\end{pmatrix},\qquad\phi_{\alpha}^{\prime}(x)\in\{\alpha,1\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Hence for any tangent vector <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A5.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|J_{E}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{v}\|^{2}=\phi_{\alpha}^{\prime}(x_{1})^{2}v_{1}^{2}+\phi_{\alpha}^{\prime}(x_{2})^{2}v_{2}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|J_{E}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{v}\|^{2}=\phi_{\alpha}^{\prime}(x_{1})^{2}v_{1}^{2}+\phi_{\alpha}^{\prime}(x_{2})^{2}v_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\alpha\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\alpha\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{2}\|\mathbf{v}\|^{2}\leq\|J_{E}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{v}\|^{2}\leq\|\mathbf{v}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{2}\|\mathbf{v}\|^{2}\leq\|J_{E}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{v}\|^{2}\leq\|\mathbf{v}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{v}\|^{2}\leq\|J_{E}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{v}\|^{2}\leq\alpha^{2}\|\mathbf{v}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{v}\|^{2}\leq\|J_{E}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{v}\|^{2}\leq\alpha^{2}\|\mathbf{v}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Substituting into Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 3.1 (Fisher Information Bounds for Bi-Lipschitz Encoders). ‣ 3.1 Global Isometry and Fisher Preservation ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> yields</p>
<table id="A5.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}\mathcal{I}^{(D)}\leq\mathcal{I}^{(d)}\leq\dfrac{1}{\alpha^{2}}\mathcal{I}^{(D)},&amp;0&lt;\alpha\leq 1,\\[6.0pt]
\dfrac{1}{\alpha^{2}}\mathcal{I}^{(D)}\leq\mathcal{I}^{(d)}\leq\mathcal{I}^{(D)},&amp;\alpha&gt;1.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}\mathcal{I}^{(D)}\leq\mathcal{I}^{(d)}\leq\dfrac{1}{\alpha^{2}}\mathcal{I}^{(D)},&amp;0&lt;\alpha\leq 1,\\[6.0pt]
\dfrac{1}{\alpha^{2}}\mathcal{I}^{(D)}\leq\mathcal{I}^{(d)}\leq\mathcal{I}^{(D)},&amp;\alpha&gt;1.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For the Leaky ReLU encoder <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\mathbf{x})=(E_{1}(\mathbf{x}),E_{2}(\mathbf{x}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\mathbf{x})=(E_{1}(\mathbf{x}),E_{2}(\mathbf{x}))</annotation></semantics></math> with negative slope <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the Hessians satisfy</p>
<table id="A5.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}^{2}E_{1}(\mathbf{x})=\begin{pmatrix}(1-\alpha)\delta(x_{1})&amp;0\\
0&amp;0\end{pmatrix},\qquad\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}^{2}E_{2}(\mathbf{x})=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;0\\
0&amp;(1-\alpha)\delta(x_{2})\end{pmatrix}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}^{2}E_{1}(\mathbf{x})=\begin{pmatrix}(1-\alpha)\delta(x_{1})&amp;0\\
0&amp;0\end{pmatrix},\qquad\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}^{2}E_{2}(\mathbf{x})=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;0\\
0&amp;(1-\alpha)\delta(x_{2})\end{pmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Dirac delta distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Thus the encoder curvature vanishes for <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{2}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{2}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, since the map is piecewise linear. At <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the second derivatives become singular through the Dirac delta terms, with magnitude proportional to <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Derivation Relevant for the Experiment in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 FIR Deviation in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a></h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide derivations for the experiment in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 FIR Deviation in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.
We consider data <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> on a 2-dimensional intrinsic manifold, embedded in <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, with latent representations <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Linear Encoder</h5>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We derive the deviation parameter <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 (Linear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> for the linear encoder used in our experiment. Take <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=d=m=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=d=m=2</annotation></semantics></math> and consider the encoder</p>
<table id="A5.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{x},\qquad\mathbf{A}=\mathrm{diag}\bigl(\sqrt{1+\delta_{0}},\,\sqrt{1-\delta_{0}}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐀𝐱</mi></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{x},\qquad\mathbf{A}=\mathrm{diag}\bigl(\sqrt{1+\delta_{0}},\,\sqrt{1-\delta_{0}}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Then</p>
<table id="A5.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}^{\top}\mathbf{A}-\mathbf{I}_{2}=\mathrm{diag}(1+\delta_{0},\;1-\delta_{0})-\mathbf{I}_{2}=\mathrm{diag}(\delta_{0},\;-\delta_{0})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐀</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}^{\top}\mathbf{A}-\mathbf{I}_{2}=\mathrm{diag}(1+\delta_{0},\;1-\delta_{0})-\mathbf{I}_{2}=\mathrm{diag}(\delta_{0},\;-\delta_{0}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Therefore,</p>
<table id="A5.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{A}^{\top}\mathbf{A}-\mathbf{I}_{2}\|_{2}=\delta_{0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐀</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{A}^{\top}\mathbf{A}-\mathbf{I}_{2}\|_{2}=\delta_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Thus, we can take</p>
<table id="A5.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\delta_{0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\delta_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dimension-Varying Encoder</h5>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide derivation of the deviation parameter <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 (Linear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.
Take <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=512</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq m=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq m=2</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, the encoder maps</p>
<table id="A5.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)\in\mathbb{R}^{d}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)\in\mathbb{R}^{d}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since the encoder acts as the identity on the intrinsic subspace, we may take</p>
<table id="A5.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}=\mathbf{I}_{m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}=\mathbf{I}_{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Therefore</p>
<table id="A5.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}^{\top}\mathbf{A}-\mathbf{I}_{m}=\mathbf{0},\qquad\|\mathbf{A}^{\top}\mathbf{A}-\mathbf{I}_{m}\|_{2}=0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐀</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐀</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}^{\top}\mathbf{A}-\mathbf{I}_{m}=\mathbf{0},\qquad\|\mathbf{A}^{\top}\mathbf{A}-\mathbf{I}_{m}\|_{2}=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">and the deviation parameter in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 (Linear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> satisfies</p>
<table id="A5.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Nonlinear Encoder</h5>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=d=3</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(y_{1},y_{2},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(y_{1},y_{2},0)</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the encoder</p>
<table id="A5.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E((x_{1},x_{2},0),\varepsilon_{0})=\left(\frac{\sin(\varepsilon_{0}x_{1})}{\varepsilon_{0}},\;x_{2},\;\frac{1-\cos(\varepsilon_{0}x_{1})}{\varepsilon_{0}}\right),\qquad\varepsilon_{0}&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E((x_{1},x_{2},0),\varepsilon_{0})=\left(\frac{\sin(\varepsilon_{0}x_{1})}{\varepsilon_{0}},\;x_{2},\;\frac{1-\cos(\varepsilon_{0}x_{1})}{\varepsilon_{0}}\right),\qquad\varepsilon_{0}&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We first provide derivation of the linear deviation parameter <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and the dimension penalty term in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Nonlinear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>.
Since <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=d</annotation></semantics></math>, the dimension-penalty term <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{D-d}{D-m}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{D-d}{D-m}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Nonlinear Encoder</h5>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=d=3</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(y_{1},y_{2},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(y_{1},y_{2},0)</annotation></semantics></math>, define the encoder</p>
<table id="A5.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E((x_{1},x_{2},0),\varepsilon_{0})=\left(\frac{\sin(\varepsilon_{0}x_{1})}{\varepsilon_{0}},\;x_{2},\;\frac{1-\cos(\varepsilon_{0}x_{1})}{\varepsilon_{0}}\right),\qquad\varepsilon_{0}&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E((x_{1},x_{2},0),\varepsilon_{0})=\left(\frac{\sin(\varepsilon_{0}x_{1})}{\varepsilon_{0}},\;x_{2},\;\frac{1-\cos(\varepsilon_{0}x_{1})}{\varepsilon_{0}}\right),\qquad\varepsilon_{0}&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first derive the linear deviation parameter <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and the dimension penalty term in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Nonlinear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>.
Since <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=d</annotation></semantics></math>, the dimension-penalty term in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Nonlinear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> satisfies</p>
<table id="A5.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{D-d}{D-m}=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{D-d}{D-m}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Next we derive the linear deviation parameter <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Nonlinear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>. Let <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\subset\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\subset\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the plane</p>
<table id="A5.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M=\{(x_{1},x_{2},0):x_{1},x_{2}\in\mathbb{R}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\{(x_{1},x_{2},0):x_{1},x_{2}\in\mathbb{R}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Then the distribution <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> supported on <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is the Gaussian measure</p>
<table id="A5.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d\mu(x_{1},x_{2})=\frac{1}{2\pi}e^{-(x_{1}^{2}+x_{2}^{2})/2}\,dx_{1}dx_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\mu(x_{1},x_{2})=\frac{1}{2\pi}e^{-(x_{1}^{2}+x_{2}^{2})/2}\,dx_{1}dx_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}=(a,b,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}=(a,b,0)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The Jacobian of <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="A5.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla E(x_{0})=\begin{pmatrix}\cos(\varepsilon_{0}a)&amp;0&amp;0\\
0&amp;1&amp;0\\
\sin(\varepsilon_{0}a)&amp;0&amp;0\end{pmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla E(x_{0})=\begin{pmatrix}\cos(\varepsilon_{0}a)&amp;0&amp;0\\
0&amp;1&amp;0\\
\sin(\varepsilon_{0}a)&amp;0&amp;0\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}=\nabla E(\mathbf{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}=\nabla E(\mathbf{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</p>
<table id="A5.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}^{\top}\mathbf{A}_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}=\begin{pmatrix}\cos^{2}(\varepsilon_{0}a)+\sin^{2}(\varepsilon_{0}a)&amp;0\\
0&amp;1\end{pmatrix}=\mathbf{I}_{m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}^{\top}\mathbf{A}_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}=\begin{pmatrix}\cos^{2}(\varepsilon_{0}a)+\sin^{2}(\varepsilon_{0}a)&amp;0\\
0&amp;1\end{pmatrix}=\mathbf{I}_{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore</p>
<table id="A5.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{A}_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}^{\top}\mathbf{A}_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}-\mathbf{I}_{m}\|_{2}=0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{A}_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}^{\top}\mathbf{A}_{\mathbf{x}_{0}}-\mathbf{I}_{m}\|_{2}=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">and the linear deviation parameter in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Nonlinear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> satisfies</p>
<table id="A5.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Lastly, we explicitly compute the nonlinear deviation parameter <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> appearing in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Nonlinear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>.
We compute the quantity in (<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Theorem 3.5 (Nonlinear Stability). ‣ 3.3 Stability Theorems on Flat Support ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).
Define the Taylor remainder</p>
<table id="A5.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{x}_{0})=E(\mathbf{x})-\big(E(\mathbf{x}_{0})+\nabla E(\mathbf{x}_{0})(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_{0})\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{x}_{0})=E(\mathbf{x})-\big(E(\mathbf{x}_{0})+\nabla E(\mathbf{x}_{0})(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_{0})\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Since the second coordinate of <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is linear, the remainder
only appears in the first and third components.
Let</p>
<table id="A5.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=\varepsilon_{0}x_{1},\qquad t_{0}=\varepsilon_{0}a." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=\varepsilon_{0}x_{1},\qquad t_{0}=\varepsilon_{0}a.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p7.2" class="ltx_p">Then</p>
<table id="A5.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.Ex60"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex60.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\sin t-\sin t_{0}-\cos t_{0}(t-t_{0})}{\varepsilon_{0}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\sin t-\sin t_{0}-\cos t_{0}(t-t_{0})}{\varepsilon_{0}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A5.Ex61"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R_{3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex61.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{(1-\cos t)-(1-\cos t_{0})-\sin t_{0}(t-t_{0})}{\varepsilon_{0}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{(1-\cos t)-(1-\cos t_{0})-\sin t_{0}(t-t_{0})}{\varepsilon_{0}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p7.3" class="ltx_p">By Taylor’s theorem with remainder,
there exist <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi,\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi,\eta</annotation></semantics></math> between <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="A5.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sin t=\sin t_{0}+\cos t_{0}(t-t_{0})-\frac{\sin\xi}{2}(t-t_{0})^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sin t=\sin t_{0}+\cos t_{0}(t-t_{0})-\frac{\sin\xi}{2}(t-t_{0})^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A5.Ex63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\cos t=1-\cos t_{0}+\sin t_{0}(t-t_{0})+\frac{\cos\eta}{2}(t-t_{0})^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\cos t=1-\cos t_{0}+\sin t_{0}(t-t_{0})+\frac{\cos\eta}{2}(t-t_{0})^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p7.4" class="ltx_p">Therefore</p>
<table id="A5.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}=-\frac{\sin\xi}{2\varepsilon_{0}}(t-t_{0})^{2},\qquad R_{3}=\frac{\cos\eta}{2\varepsilon_{0}}(t-t_{0})^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}=-\frac{\sin\xi}{2\varepsilon_{0}}(t-t_{0})^{2},\qquad R_{3}=\frac{\cos\eta}{2\varepsilon_{0}}(t-t_{0})^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p7.5" class="ltx_p">Using <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\sin|\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\sin|\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\cos|\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\cos|\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain</p>
<table id="A5.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|R_{1}|\leq\frac{1}{2\varepsilon_{0}}(t-t_{0})^{2},\qquad|R_{3}|\leq\frac{1}{2\varepsilon_{0}}(t-t_{0})^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|R_{1}|\leq\frac{1}{2\varepsilon_{0}}(t-t_{0})^{2},\qquad|R_{3}|\leq\frac{1}{2\varepsilon_{0}}(t-t_{0})^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p7.6" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-t_{0}=\varepsilon_{0}(x_{1}-a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-t_{0}=\varepsilon_{0}(x_{1}-a)</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A5.Ex66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|R_{1}|\leq\frac{\varepsilon_{0}}{2}(x_{1}-a)^{2},\qquad|R_{3}|\leq\frac{\varepsilon_{0}}{2}(x_{1}-a)^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|R_{1}|\leq\frac{\varepsilon_{0}}{2}(x_{1}-a)^{2},\qquad|R_{3}|\leq\frac{\varepsilon_{0}}{2}(x_{1}-a)^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p7.7" class="ltx_p">Consequently</p>
<table id="A5.Ex67" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|R(x,x_{0})\|^{2}=R_{1}^{2}+R_{3}^{2}\leq\frac{\varepsilon_{0}^{2}}{2}(x_{1}-a)^{4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|R(x,x_{0})\|^{2}=R_{1}^{2}+R_{3}^{2}\leq\frac{\varepsilon_{0}^{2}}{2}(x_{1}-a)^{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the fourth moment is <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[X_{1}^{4}]=3." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[X_{1}^{4}]=3.</annotation></semantics></math> A direct computation gives</p>
<table id="A5.Ex68" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[(X_{1}-a)^{4}]=3+6a^{2}+a^{4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[(X_{1}-a)^{4}]=3+6a^{2}+a^{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Therefore</p>
<table id="A5.Ex69" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{M}\|E(\mathbf{x})-(E(\mathbf{x}_{0})+\nabla E(\mathbf{x}_{0})(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_{0}))\|^{2}d\mu(\mathbf{x})\leq\frac{\varepsilon_{0}^{2}}{2}(3+6a^{2}+a^{4})=:\varepsilon^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{M}\|E(\mathbf{x})-(E(\mathbf{x}_{0})+\nabla E(\mathbf{x}_{0})(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_{0}))\|^{2}d\mu(\mathbf{x})\leq\frac{\varepsilon_{0}^{2}}{2}(3+6a^{2}+a^{4})=:\varepsilon^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p8.3" class="ltx_p">The right hand side is minimized when <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=0</annotation></semantics></math>, thus <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon=\sqrt{\frac{3}{2}}\;\varepsilon_{0}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msqrt><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon=\sqrt{\frac{3}{2}}\;\varepsilon_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.3 </span>Supplementary Plots</h3>

<div id="A5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 FIR Deviation in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4.2 FIR Deviation in the Gaussian Toy Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents the FIR deviation <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> as functions of the parameters <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for fixed noise variance <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. Here we provide additional plots that instead show the dependence of <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> for the same experimental settings. These results are shown in Figures <a href="#A5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ E.3 Supplementary Plots ‣ Appendix E Gaussian Toy Experiment: Derivations and Additional Results ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, <a href="#A5.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ E.3 Supplementary Plots ‣ Appendix E Gaussian Toy Experiment: Derivations and Additional Results ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, and <a href="#A5.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ E.3 Supplementary Plots ‣ Appendix E Gaussian Toy Experiment: Derivations and Additional Results ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A5.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/gaussian_FIR_deviation_delta.png" id="A5.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/288;" width="476" height="288" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A5.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/gaussian_FIR_deviation_delta2.png" id="A5.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/289;" width="476" height="289" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>
FIR deviation <math id="A5.F7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. (a) <math id="A5.F7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and (b) <math id="A5.F7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in toy settings.
Data <math id="A5.F7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> are embedded as <math id="A5.F7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and encoded to <math id="A5.F7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider <math id="A5.F7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=d=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=d=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.F7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐀𝐱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A5.F7.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}=\mathrm{diag}(\sqrt{1+\delta_{0}},\sqrt{1-\delta_{0}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}=\mathrm{diag}(\sqrt{1+\delta_{0}},\sqrt{1-\delta_{0}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We compute <math id="A5.F7.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> from
<math id="A5.F7.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.F7.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(d)}((\mu_{Z})_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(d)}((\mu_{Z})_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math>
using diffusion models trained on <math id="A5.F7.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.F7.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math>. Solid line <math id="A5.F7.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1.25\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1.25</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1.25\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in (b) serve as linear references.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A5.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A5.F8.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/gaussian_FIR_deviation_d.png" id="A5.F8.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/290;" width="476" height="290" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A5.F8.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/gaussian_FIR_deviation_d2.png" id="A5.F8.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/292;" width="476" height="292" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>
FIR deviation <math id="A5.F8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. (a) <math id="A5.F8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and (b) <math id="A5.F8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> in toy settings.
Data <math id="A5.F8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> are embedded as <math id="A5.F8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A5.F8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=512</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="A5.F8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We compute <math id="A5.F8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> from
<math id="A5.F8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.F8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(d)}((\mu_{Z})_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(d)}((\mu_{Z})_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math>
using diffusion models trained on <math id="A5.F8.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.F8.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math>. Solid line <math id="A5.F8.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=\frac{D-d}{D-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=\frac{D-d}{D-2}</annotation></semantics></math> in (b) serve as linear references.
</figcaption>
</figure>
