<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A5.F9.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/gaussian_FIR_deviation_epsilon.png" id="A5.F9.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/290;" width="476" height="290" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A5.F9.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/gaussian_FIR_deviation_epsilon2.png" id="A5.F9.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/292;" width="476" height="292" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>
FIR deviation <math id="A5.F9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. (a) <math id="A5.F9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and (b) <math id="A5.F9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in toy settings.
Data <math id="A5.F9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> are embedded as <math id="A5.F9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(y_{1},y_{2},0,\ldots,0)\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and encoded to <math id="A5.F9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=E(\mathbf{x})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider <math id="A5.F9.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=d=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.F9.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E((x_{1},x_{2},0),\varepsilon_{0})=\bigl(\sin(\varepsilon_{0}x_{1})/\varepsilon_{0},\;x_{2},\;(1-\cos(\varepsilon_{0}x_{1}))/\varepsilon_{0}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E((x_{1},x_{2},0),\varepsilon_{0})=\bigl(\sin(\varepsilon_{0}x_{1})/\varepsilon_{0},\;x_{2},\;(1-\cos(\varepsilon_{0}x_{1}))/\varepsilon_{0}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We compute <math id="A5.F9.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> from
<math id="A5.F9.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.F9.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(d)}((\mu_{Z})_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(d)}((\mu_{Z})_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math>
using diffusion models trained on <math id="A5.F9.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.F9.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math>.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Bi-Lipschitz Constant Estimation for GPE and VAE</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the GPE and VAE models trained on FFHQ used in the experiments of Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, we estimate the bi-Lipschitz constants and the corresponding values of <math id="A6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> used in the FI bound of Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 3.1 (Fisher Information Bounds for Bi-Lipschitz Encoders). ‣ 3.1 Global Isometry and Fisher Preservation ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 3.1 (Fisher Information Bounds for Bi-Lipschitz Encoders). ‣ 3.1 Global Isometry and Fisher Preservation ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, the constants <math id="A6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c,C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c,C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> are defined through the Jacobian bounds</p>
<table id="A6.Ex70" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\,\|\mathbf{v}\|\leq\|J_{E}(\mathbf{x})\,\mathbf{v}\|\leq C\,\|\mathbf{v}\|,\quad\forall\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{M},\ \forall\mathbf{v}\in T_{\mathbf{x}}\mathcal{M}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\,\|\mathbf{v}\|\leq\|J_{E}(\mathbf{x})\,\mathbf{v}\|\leq C\,\|\mathbf{v}\|,\quad\forall\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{M},\ \forall\mathbf{v}\in T_{\mathbf{x}}\mathcal{M}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">To estimate these constants in practice, we use the global distance distortion of the encoder:</p>
<table id="A6.Ex71" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{c}\|\mathbf{x}_{1}-\mathbf{x}_{2}\|\leq\|E(\mathbf{x}_{1})-E(\mathbf{x}_{2})\|\leq\hat{C}\|\mathbf{x}_{1}-\mathbf{x}_{2}\|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{c}\|\mathbf{x}_{1}-\mathbf{x}_{2}\|\leq\|E(\mathbf{x}_{1})-E(\mathbf{x}_{2})\|\leq\hat{C}\|\mathbf{x}_{1}-\mathbf{x}_{2}\|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p2.3" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is differentiable, this global bi-Lipschitz bound implies
<math id="A6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{c}\|\mathbf{v}\|\leq\|J_{E}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{v}\|\leq\hat{C}\|\mathbf{v}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{c}\|\mathbf{v}\|\leq\|J_{E}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{v}\|\leq\hat{C}\|\mathbf{v}\|</annotation></semantics></math>
for all <math id="A6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and tangent vectors <math id="A6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="A6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{c},\hat{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{c},\hat{C})</annotation></semantics></math> provide valid estimates for <math id="A6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,C)</annotation></semantics></math> in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 3.1 (Fisher Information Bounds for Bi-Lipschitz Encoders). ‣ 3.1 Global Isometry and Fisher Preservation ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To estimate <math id="A6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{c},\hat{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{c},\hat{C})</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample <math id="A6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5000</annotation></semantics></math> images <math id="A6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{x}_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{x}_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> from the FFHQ dataset and compute their encoded representations <math id="A6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{E(\mathbf{x}_{i})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{E(\mathbf{x}_{i})\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each pair <math id="A6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>, we evaluate the distortion ratio</p>
<table id="A6.Ex72" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{ij}=\frac{\|E(\mathbf{x}_{i})-E(\mathbf{x}_{j})\|}{\|\mathbf{x}_{i}-\mathbf{x}_{j}\|}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{ij}=\frac{\|E(\mathbf{x}_{i})-E(\mathbf{x}_{j})\|}{\|\mathbf{x}_{i}-\mathbf{x}_{j}\|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Using all pairs, we estimate the constants as</p>
<table id="A6.Ex73" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{c}\approx\min_{i&lt;j}r_{ij},\qquad\hat{C}\approx\max_{i&lt;j}r_{ij}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>≈</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>≈</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{c}\approx\min_{i&lt;j}r_{ij},\qquad\hat{C}\approx\max_{i&lt;j}r_{ij}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p3.3" class="ltx_p">The resulting estimates are reported in Table <a href="#A6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Appendix F Bi-Lipschitz Constant Estimation for GPE and VAE ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, together with the ratio <math id="A6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}/\hat{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}/\hat{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, which provides an empirical estimate of the bi-Lipschitz constant.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A6.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A6.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Model</th>
<th id="A6.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="A6.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{c}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A6.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="A6.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A6.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="A6.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}/\hat{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}/\hat{c}</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A6.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">GPE</th>
<td id="A6.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.88</td>
<td id="A6.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">1.04</td>
<td id="A6.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">1.18</td></tr>
<tr id="A6.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">VAE</th>
<td id="A6.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.20</td>
<td id="A6.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">3.86</td>
<td id="A6.T1.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">19.67</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Empirical estimates <math id="A6.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{c},\hat{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{c},\hat{C})</annotation></semantics></math> of the constants <math id="A6.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,C)</annotation></semantics></math> in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 3.1 (Fisher Information Bounds for Bi-Lipschitz Encoders). ‣ 3.1 Global Isometry and Fisher Preservation ‣ 3 Analysis of Diffusability via Fisher Information ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> for encoders of the GPE and VAE trained on FFHQ. The ratio <math id="A6.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}/\hat{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}/\hat{c}</annotation></semantics></math> provides an empirical estimate of the bi-Lipschitz constant.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Additional Model Performance Results</h2>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section reports additional quantitative results for the models used in the experiments in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.
Table <a href="#A7.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Appendix G Additional Model Performance Results ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> summarizes the reconstruction MSE of the GPE and VAE autoencoders trained on FFHQ, together with the FID scores of diffusion models trained in pixel space and on the corresponding latent representations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A7.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>
Generation quality and reconstruction accuracy on FFHQ.
FID (Fréchet Inception Distance) measures the quality of generated images.
For the pixel model, images are generated by sampling a diffusion model trained directly on FFHQ.
For the GPE and VAE models, images are generated by sampling diffusion models trained in the latent space and decoding the generated latents using the corresponding decoder. The diffusion models use the DiT architecture within the EDM framework.
Reconstruction performance for the GPE and VAE trained on FFHQ is reported as mean squared error (MSE).
</figcaption>
<table id="A7.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A7.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A7.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="A7.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></th>
<th id="A7.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="A7.T2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generation FID</span></th>
<th id="A7.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="A7.T2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reconstruction MSE</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A7.T2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A7.T2.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">Pixel Diffusion</th>
<td id="A7.T2.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">3.04</td>
<td id="A7.T2.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">–</td></tr>
<tr id="A7.T2.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A7.T2.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">GPE (Latent Diffusion)</th>
<td id="A7.T2.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">74.36</td>
<td id="A7.T2.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">0.0018</td></tr>
<tr id="A7.T2.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A7.T2.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">VAE (Latent Diffusion)</th>
<td id="A7.T2.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">56.05</td>
<td id="A7.T2.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">0.014</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A8" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix H </span>FFHQ Experiments Using the EDM U-Net Architecture</h2>

<div id="A8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section reports additional results for the experiments in
Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> and Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ Data with NVAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> using the original
EDM U-Net architecture provided in the official EDM implementation, instead of the DiT backbone.</p>
</div>
<section id="A8.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">H.1 </span>FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding</h3>

<section id="A8.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">H.1.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h4>

<div id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The experimental setup largely follows that of Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.
We use the same pretrained GPE and VAE models trained on the FFHQ dataset and follow the same procedures for both the pixel-space and latent-space experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The main difference lies in the diffusion model architecture.
Instead of the DiT backbone used in the main experiments, we employ the original convolutional U-Net architecture within the EDM framework provided in the official implementation<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              <a href="https://github.com/NVlabs/edm" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/NVlabs/edm</a></span></span></span>.
For the pixel-space experiment, where the diffusion model is trained directly on FFHQ images at <math id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math> resolution, we use the pretrained checkpoint provided in the official EDM repository.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the latent-space experiments, diffusion models are trained on the GPE and VAE latent representations using the same U-Net architecture and a similar training configuration as the pixel-based model, with residual block configuration <math id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2,2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2,2,2)</annotation></semantics></math> across the resolution levels.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For training, we use a batch size of <math id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>256</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256</annotation></semantics></math>, dropout rate <math id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05</annotation></semantics></math>, and a learning-rate schedule consisting of a <math id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> kimg warmup followed by a constant learning rate of <math id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Optimization is performed using Adam with <math id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}=0.999" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.999</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}=0.999</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=10^{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=10^{-8}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Models are trained for <math id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> kimg.
The pretrained pixel-space model uses data augmentation with probability <math id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.15</annotation></semantics></math>, while no data augmentation is applied when training diffusion models on the latent representations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For sampling, we follow the standard EDM procedure with <math id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> steps, <math id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\min}=0.002" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.002</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\min}=0.002</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}=80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>80</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}=80</annotation></semantics></math>.
We set <math id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{churn}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>churn</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{churn}}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for the pixel-space and VAE latent models, and <math id="A8.SS1.SSS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{churn}}=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>churn</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{churn}}=20</annotation></semantics></math> for the GPE latent model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A8.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">H.1.2 </span>Results</h4>

<div id="A8.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A8.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ H.1.2 Results ‣ H.1 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ Appendix H FFHQ Experiments Using the EDM U-Net Architecture ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> shows representative samples generated by diffusion models trained on pixel-space FFHQ images as well as on the latent representations produced by GPE and VAE.
Table <a href="#A8.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ H.1.2 Results ‣ H.1 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ Appendix H FFHQ Experiments Using the EDM U-Net Architecture ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> reports the FID scores of diffusion models trained on pixel-space images and on the corresponding latent representations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A8.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ H.1.2 Results ‣ H.1 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ Appendix H FFHQ Experiments Using the EDM U-Net Architecture ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>(a) and (b) compare the FI and FIR for models trained in pixel space and on the latent representations of GPE and VAE, while Figure <a href="#A8.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ H.1.2 Results ‣ H.1 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ Appendix H FFHQ Experiments Using the EDM U-Net Architecture ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>(c) shows the scaled FIR deviation.
In computing the scaling factor, we set <math id="A8.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=3\times 64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=3\times 64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math>, the dimension of the pixel space, and <math id="A8.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=256</annotation></semantics></math>, the latent dimension used for both GPE and VAE.
Overall, these results are consistent with those reported in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> using diffusion models with the DiT architecture.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A8.F10" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="A8.F10.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/EDM_conv/EDM_gen_images.png" id="A8.F10.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="A8.F10.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/EDM_conv/GPE_gen_images.png" id="A8.F10.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span></figcaption>
