</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 11: </span>
Values of (a) <math id="A8.F11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> and (b) <math id="A8.F11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> plotted versus the noise variance <math id="A8.F11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, computed from diffusion models trained on different data representations. The diffusion models use the U-Net architecture within the EDM framework. The <em id="A8.F11.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pixel</em> curves correspond to models trained directly on FFHQ images. The <em id="A8.F11.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">latent</em> curves correspond to models trained on latent representations of an image encoder (GPE or VAE) pretrained on FFHQ. We show <math id="A8.F11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\tau}\in[0.01,80]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>τ</mi></msqrt><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.01</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>80</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\tau}\in[0.01,80]</annotation></semantics></math>, excluding smaller <math id="A8.F11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> due to numerical instability. 
<br class="ltx_break">(c) Scaled FIR deviation <math id="A8.F11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>sc</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{sc}}</annotation></semantics></math> versus <math id="A8.F11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, computed from
<math id="A8.F11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(D)}(\mu_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A8.F11.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{(d)}((\mu_{Z})_{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{(d)}((\mu_{Z})_{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from the pixel-based and latent-based diffusion models described above.
Here <math id="A8.F11.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=64\times 64\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=64\times 64\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A8.F11.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=256</annotation></semantics></math>.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A8.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">H.2 </span>FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ Data with NVAE encoding</h3>

<section id="A8.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">H.2.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h4>

<div id="A8.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The experimental setup largely follows that of Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ Data with NVAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>.
We use the same pretrained NVAE model trained on the FFHQ dataset and follow the same experimental procedure.
The main difference is that the diffusion models here use the original convolutional U-Net architecture within the EDM framework, instead of the DiT backbone used in the main experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the pixel-space experiments, we use the same diffusion model trained on FFHQ data as described in Appendix <a href="#A8.SS1" title="H.1 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ Appendix H FFHQ Experiments Using the EDM U-Net Architecture ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the latent-space experiments, we train separate diffusion models on the NVAE latent representations at each spatial resolution <math id="A8.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{z}}\in\{32,16,8,4,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{z}}\in\{32,16,8,4,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The diffusion architecture and training setup follow those described in the previous subsection <a href="#A8.SS1" title="H.1 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ Appendix H FFHQ Experiments Using the EDM U-Net Architecture ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.1</span></a>, except for small latent resolutions where we slightly simplify the U-Net: for <math id="A8.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{z}}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{z}}=4</annotation></semantics></math> we use residual block configuration <math id="A8.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>, and for <math id="A8.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{z}}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{z}}=2</annotation></semantics></math> we use <math id="A8.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>, due to the limited spatial resolution of the inputs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A8.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">H.2.2 </span>Results</h4>

<div id="A8.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A8.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ H.2.2 Results ‣ H.2 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ Data with NVAE encoding ‣ Appendix H FFHQ Experiments Using the EDM U-Net Architecture ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> compares the FI and FIR computed from diffusion models trained directly in pixel space with those obtained from models trained on NVAE latent representations at different spatial resolutions <math id="A8.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The results are consistent with those reported in Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ Data with NVAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> using the diffusion model with the DiT architecture.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A8.F12" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A8.F12.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/EDM_conv/NVAE_FI.png" id="A8.F12.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/289;" width="476" height="289" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A8.F12.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/EDM_conv/NVAE_FIR.png" id="A8.F12.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/289;" width="476" height="289" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 12: </span>
Values of (a) <math id="A8.F12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> and (b) <math id="A8.F12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> plotted versus the noise variance <math id="A8.F12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> for diffusion models trained on different data representations. The diffusion models use the U-Net architecture within the EDM framework.
<em id="A8.F12.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Pixel</em> and <em id="A8.F12.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">latent</em> curves denote models trained on FFHQ images and their pretrained NVAE latents, respectively. NVAE was pretrained on FFHQ, with spatial size <math id="A8.F12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20\times d_{\mathbf{z}}\times d_{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\times d_{\mathbf{z}}\times d_{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A8.F12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> indicated in the legend).
We show <math id="A8.F12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\tau}\in[0.01,80]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>τ</mi></msqrt><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.01</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>80</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\tau}\in[0.01,80]</annotation></semantics></math>, excluding smaller <math id="A8.F12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> due to numerical instability.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A9" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix I </span>Spectra and Connection to Fisher Geometry</h2>

<div id="A9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the main text, we introduce Fisher information (FI) and Fisher information rate (FIR) as tools for analyzing the diffusability of autoencoder representations.
In this section, we examine spectral analysis as an alternative perspective for studying diffusability. Corresponding to the experiments in Sections <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ Data with NVAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>, which compare FI and FIR between pixel space and latent space, we analyze the power spectra of FFHQ images and of latent representations produced by the GPE, VAE, and NVAE encoders. This analysis provides a complementary view of the signal structure and allows us to contrast the information revealed by spectral behavior with that captured by FI and FIR.</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We use the same <math id="A9.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math> resolution FFHQ images and the same NVAE, GPE, and VAE models trained on FFHQ data as in the experiments in Sections <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ Data with NVAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>. For FFHQ images and the NVAE latent representations, which are two-dimensional spatial tensors, we compute the two-dimensional power spectrum by applying the Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) over the spatial dimensions. For multi-channel inputs, the resulting spectra are averaged across channels to obtain a single spectrum per sample.</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the latent representations produced by the GPE and VAE encoders, the latent variable for each sample is a one-dimensional vector. To analyze their frequency characteristics, we compute the one-dimensional power spectrum using the discrete Fourier transform (FFT) along the latent dimension. Each latent vector is normalized prior to the transform.</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Both the frequency axis <math id="A9.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and the power values <math id="A9.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> are normalized by the signal length (or spatial resolution for the 2D case).
To obtain a stable estimate, the spectrum is averaged over <math id="A9.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> samples drawn from the dataset.
The zero-frequency (DC) component corresponding to the mean signal level is excluded from the plots.</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A9.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ Appendix I Spectra and Connection to Fisher Geometry ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>(a) shows the power spectrum of FFHQ images.
Figures (b)–(d) show the spectra of NVAE latent representations at different spatial resolutions.
Although these spectra also decrease with frequency, their shapes differ from the pixel-space spectrum in (a), and the decay behavior varies across resolutions, indicating a mismatch with the spectral structure of the original images.
Figures (e) and (f) show the spectra of the GPE and VAE latent representations.
Unlike the pixel spectrum in (a), these spectra exhibit strong oscillations and lack a clear monotonic decay, further deviating from the spectral behavior of the original images.</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, the latent spectra do not always align with the spectrum of the original images. This suggests that spectral statistics alone may not be the most reliable diagnostic for assessing diffusability. In contrast, the FI and FIR analysis in Sections <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ with GPE and VAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 FI and FIR Comparison on FFHQ Data with NVAE encoding ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Understanding Latent Diffusability via Fisher Geometry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> provides a more consistent characterization of the diffusability of the corresponding representations.
We also note that spectral statistics are not permutation invariant: for example, randomly shuffling the pixels of an image would destroy its spectrum by removing the spatial structure on which the Fourier transform relies. In contrast, FI and FIR depend on the geometry of the probability density through the score norm <math id="A9.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p(\mathbf{x})\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p(\mathbf{x})\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and are therefore invariant under permutations of coordinates.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A9.F13" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A9.F13.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/image_spectrum.png" id="A9.F13.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/289;" width="476" height="289" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>FFHQ Images</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A9.F13.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/NVAE_latent32_spectrum.png" id="A9.F13.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/289;" width="476" height="289" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>NVAE latents (<math id="A9.F13.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{z}=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{z}=32</annotation></semantics></math>)</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A9.F13.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/NVAE_latent16_spectrum.png" id="A9.F13.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/289;" width="476" height="289" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>NVAE latents (<math id="A9.F13.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{z}=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{z}=16</annotation></semantics></math>)</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A9.F13.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/NVAE_latent8_spectrum.png" id="A9.F13.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/288;" width="476" height="288" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(d) </span>NVAE latents (<math id="A9.F13.sf4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{z}=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{z}=8</annotation></semantics></math>)</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A9.F13.sf5" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/GPE_spectrum.png" id="A9.F13.sf5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/289;" width="476" height="289" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(e) </span>GPE latents</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A9.F13.sf6" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2604.02751v1/images/VAE_spectrum.png" id="A9.F13.sf6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/289;" width="476" height="289" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(f) </span>VAE latents</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 13: </span>Power spectra of FFHQ images and latent representations.
(a) Power spectrum of FFHQ images.
(b)–(d) Power spectra of NVAE latent representations with spatial resolutions <math id="A9.F13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{z}=32,16," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{z}=32,16,</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A9.F13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>.
(e)–(f) Power spectra of GPE and VAE latent representations. The NVAE, GPE, and VAE models are pretrained on FFHQ images.
For FFHQ images and NVAE latents, the spectrum is computed using the two-dimensional FFT over the spatial dimensions and averaged over channels.
For the one-dimensional latent vectors produced by the GPE and VAE encoders, the spectrum is computed using a one-dimensional FFT along the latent dimension.
The spectra are averaged over <math id="A9.F13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> samples drawn from the dataset.
Both the frequency axis <math id="A9.F13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and the power values <math id="A9.F13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> are normalized by the signal length (or spatial resolution for the 2D case).
The zero-frequency (DC) component corresponding to the mean signal level is excluded.</figcaption>
</figure>
